`)}}}},sje=["BS-FN-1"],rje=["Objectives of financial management","Efficiency and the collection of accounts receivable","Liquidity and meeting short-term debts","Solvency and reliance on long-term debt","Growth of the asset base","Short-term versus long-term financial objectives"],lje=["Efficiency","Growth","Liquidity","Solvency"],mN=[{stem:"A plumbing wholesaler allows trade customers 30 days to pay their invoices. By sending reminders on day 20, it has cut the average time customers take to pay from 41 days to 27 days.",reason:"Efficiency is the ability of the business to minimise costs and manage its assets, such as accounts receivable, to get the best return. Collecting money from customers faster, within the allowed terms, means less cash is tied up in debtors. This is measured by the accounts receivable turnover ratio.",whyNot:"Faster collection does help cash flow, but the change is about managing an asset (debtors) better rather than holding cash to pay bills. Nothing is said about long-term debt or expanding the business, so solvency and growth do not fit."},{stem:"A bakery chain reinvests its profits to open three new outlets and buy a larger commercial oven, increasing both its sales and the value of its assets.",reason:"Growth is the business's ability to increase its size in the longer term, through a larger asset base, more sales and greater market share. Opening outlets and buying equipment funded by profits is growth.",whyNot:"Nothing suggests the bakery is collecting debts faster, which rules out efficiency. It is not setting aside cash to pay short-term bills, so liquidity does not fit, and debt is not being reduced, so solvency does not fit."},{stem:"A landscaping business keeps a cash reserve and arranges a bank overdraft so it can pay wages and suppliers on time during the quiet winter months.",reason:"Liquidity is the ability to pay short-term debts as they fall due, usually within 12 months. A cash reserve and an overdraft for paying wages and suppliers during a slow season aim to protect liquidity.",whyNot:"Solvency is about meeting long-term debts, but wages and supplier bills are short-term. The business is not expanding, and it is not changing how quickly it collects from customers, so growth and efficiency do not fit."},{stem:"A transport company uses retained profits to repay a large part of its ten-year bank loan, so less of the business is now financed by borrowed funds.",reason:"Solvency is the ability to meet long-term financial commitments. Repaying a long-term loan reduces reliance on debt compared with equity, which lowers the gearing ratio and improves solvency.",whyNot:"Liquidity is about short-term debts, but this loan runs for ten years. Using profits to repay debt rather than buy new assets is not growth, and nothing is said about managing receivables or costs, so efficiency does not fit."}],cje={id:"business_studies/2025/q09",version:1,variants:mN.length,syllabusTopics:[...sje],learningTopics:rje,generate(t){const e=mN[t];return{question:[e.stem,"","Which objective of financial management is the business mainly pursuing?"].join(`
