`)}}}},yje=["BS-OP-5"],bje=["Overcoming resistance to change in operations","Financial costs of change: new equipment, retraining and redundancy","Fear of the unknown as a cause of employee resistance"],wje=["Cost of purchasing new equipment","Fear of the unknown","Redundancy payments","Retraining costs"],$N=[{stem:"The owner of a dental clinic likes the idea of replacing plaster moulds with 3D digital scanners. She keeps postponing the change because the scanners would cost \\$140 000 to buy.",reason:"Buying new equipment is a large up-front financial cost, and it can make owners and managers reluctant to adopt new technology. The only obstacle mentioned is the purchase price of the scanners.",whyNot:"No jobs are being cut, so redundancy payments do not apply. The staff are not being retrained and no anxiety among employees is mentioned, which rules out retraining costs and fear of the unknown."},{stem:"A warehouse announces that voice-directed headsets will replace paper pick lists next month. Many pickers say they are anxious because they do not know how their tasks or performance targets will change.",reason:"Employees often resist change when they are uncertain how it will affect their jobs, skills or security. The pickers do not know what their tasks or targets will be, which is fear of the unknown. Clear communication, consultation and early training can reduce it.",whyNot:"The scenario is about how employees feel, not about a cost the business must pay. The price of the headsets, redundancy payments and retraining costs are all financial reasons that may make managers resist change."},{stem:"The owners of a printing business are considering automating the binding department. They delay the decision after calculating how much the eight long-serving binding staff would be owed if their jobs were abolished.",reason:"When technology replaces jobs, the business must pay redundancy entitlements, which grow with length of service. The large amount owed to long-serving staff whose jobs would be abolished is what is holding the owners back.",whyNot:"The cost of the automated equipment itself is not what causes the delay. The staff are losing their jobs rather than being retrained, and the resistance comes from the owners, not from anxious employees."},{stem:"An accounting firm delays moving to new cloud-based accounting software. All 60 staff would need several days of paid courses, and clients would be served more slowly while everyone learns the new system.",reason:"Retraining costs include course fees, wages paid during training and the lost productivity while staff learn. These costs can discourage a business from introducing new technology. Several days of paid courses and slower client service are retraining costs.",whyNot:"Nobody is losing their job, so redundancy payments do not apply. The obstacle is training staff rather than buying equipment, and the firm, not its employees, is hesitating, which rules out fear of the unknown."}],vje={id:"business_studies/2025/q12",version:1,variants:$N.length,syllabusTopics:[...yje],learningTopics:bje,generate(t){const e=$N[t];return{question:[e.stem,"","Which source of resistance to change is most evident?"].join(`
