`)}}}},zje=["BS-FN-3"],Dje=["Limitations of financial reports","Normalised earnings and one-off items","Capitalising expenses","Valuing assets, including intangible assets","Timing issues in financial reporting"],Wje=["Capitalising expenses","Normalised earnings","Timing issues","Valuing assets"],kN=[{stem:"A trucking company spends \\$400 000 on staff time to develop its own route-planning software. Instead of recording this as an expense, it lists the amount as a non-current asset on its balance sheet, and this year's reported profit is higher as a result.",reason:"Capitalising expenses means recording spending as an asset instead of as an expense. This makes current profit look higher and the asset base larger, which may mislead users of the reports. Treating the \\$400 000 of development costs as an asset is capitalising expenses.",whyNot:"The profit is not inflated by a one-off gain, so normalised earnings do not apply. The issue is how the spending was classified, not when the reporting period ends, which rules out timing issues. Choosing to record an expense as an asset is a different problem from estimating what an existing asset is worth."},{stem:"A hardware chain's net profit this year includes a \\$3 million gain from selling its old head-office building. Investors comparing this year's profit with earlier years could think trading performance had improved sharply.",reason:"Normalised earnings are profits with one-off, abnormal items removed, so that the business's ongoing performance can be compared from year to year. The gain from selling the building will not recur, so reported profit overstates normal trading results. This is the normalised earnings limitation.",whyNot:"No spending has been wrongly recorded as an asset, so capitalising expenses does not fit. The gain has already been realised in cash, so estimating the value of an asset is not the issue. Nothing suggests the reporting date distorts the figures, which rules out timing issues."},{stem:"A ski-hire business ends its financial year on 31 August, just after its peak winter trading. Its reports show high cash and strong sales, but for most of the year its cash balance is far lower.",reason:"Timing issues arise because financial reports show the position at one date or over one period, which may not represent the rest of the year. Reporting straight after the peak season makes cash and sales look stronger than they usually are. This is a timing issue.",whyNot:"No asset is hard to value and no spending has been recorded as an asset. There is no one-off gain to strip out either, since the strong results come from normal seasonal trading, so normalised earnings do not apply."},{stem:"A winery's balance sheet includes its brand name at an estimated \\$5 million and its vineyard land at the price paid for it 25 years ago.",reason:"Valuing assets is a limitation because many values in the balance sheet are estimates or historical costs. An intangible asset such as a brand is hard to value, and land recorded at its purchase price 25 years ago may be worth far more today. Both figures may differ from what the assets would actually sell for.",whyNot:"The problem is the value placed on assets the business already owns, not a decision to record a period's expenses as an asset. No one-off gain is distorting profit, and the reporting date is not the issue, so normalised earnings and timing issues do not fit."}],Uje={id:"business_studies/2025/q18",version:1,variants:kN.length,syllabusTopics:[...zje],learningTopics:Dje,generate(t){const e=kN[t];return{question:[e.stem,"","Which limitation of financial reports does this illustrate?"].join(`
