`),choices:a,sampleAnswer:{correctChoiceIndex:e,explanation:["The pH at the equivalence point tells you what kind of salt is formed, and the starting pH tells you how strong the base is.","","The equivalence point is acidic (pH ≈ 5). At that point the solution contains only the salt, so the salt must contain a weakly acidic ion. This happens when a **weak base** reacts with a **strong acid**. A starting pH of about 11 also fits a weak base: a strong base such as $\\mathrm{NaOH}$ at a typical concentration would start near pH 13.","","$\\mathrm{NH_3}$ is a weak base and $\\mathrm{HCl}$ is a strong acid. The product $\\mathrm{NH_4^+}$ is a weak acid that donates protons to water, giving a solution with pH ≈ 5. This matches the curve.","","$$\\mathrm{NH_4^+(aq) + H_2O(l) \\rightleftharpoons NH_3(aq) + H_3O^+(aq)}$$","","The other reactions do not fit: $\\mathrm{NaOH + HCl}$ (strong base, strong acid) has an equivalence point at pH 7; $\\mathrm{NH_3 + CH_3COOH}$ (weak base, weak acid) has an equivalence point near pH 7 and no steep drop; $\\mathrm{NaOH + CH_3COOH}$ (strong base, weak acid) has a basic equivalence point."].join(`
