`),parts:[{question:"Identify W and draw its structural formula. Justify your answer using its chemical behaviour and at least THREE of the sets of spectroscopic data.",sampleAnswer:["W is **methyl propanoate**, $\\mathrm{CH_3CH_2COOCH_3}$:",jt("CCC(=O)OC",220,160),"**Chemical behaviour.** W is neutral and does not release $\\mathrm{CO_2}$ from $\\mathrm{NaHCO_3}$, so it is not a carboxylic acid. A fruity smell is typical of esters.","","**Mass spectrum.** The molecular ion at $m/z = 88$ gives $M = 88\\ \\mathrm{g\\,mol^{-1}}$, which matches $\\mathrm{C_4H_8O_2}$: $4(12.01) + 8(1.008) + 2(16.00) = 88.10$. The fragments fit this structure: $59$ is $\\mathrm{COOCH_3^+}$, $57$ is $\\mathrm{CH_3CH_2CO^+}$ and $29$ is $\\mathrm{CH_3CH_2^+}$.","","**Infrared.** The sharp peak at $1740\\ \\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ is a $\\mathrm{C{=}O}$ stretch, and the peak near $1200\\ \\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ is a $\\mathrm{C{-}O}$ stretch, together indicating an ester. The absence of a broad band at $2500$–$3500\\ \\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ rules out an $\\mathrm{O{-}H}$ group (no acid or alcohol).","","**$^{13}\\mathrm{C}$ NMR.** Four peaks mean four carbon environments, so every carbon is different. $174\\ \\mathrm{ppm}$ is the ester $\\mathrm{C{=}O}$ carbon, $51\\ \\mathrm{ppm}$ is a carbon bonded to oxygen ($\\mathrm{O{-}CH_3}$), and $27$ and $9\\ \\mathrm{ppm}$ are the $\\mathrm{CH_2}$ and $\\mathrm{CH_3}$ of an ethyl group.","","**$^1\\mathrm{H}$ NMR.** Three environments in the ratio $3:2:3$ account for all 8 hydrogens. The singlet ($3\\mathrm{H}$, $3.7\\ \\mathrm{ppm}$) is a $\\mathrm{CH_3}$ with no neighbouring H, shifted downfield because it is bonded to the ester oxygen. The quartet ($2\\mathrm{H}$) and triplet ($3\\mathrm{H}$) are an ethyl group, $\\mathrm{CH_3CH_2{-}}$. Its $\\mathrm{CH_2}$ at $2.3\\ \\mathrm{ppm}$ sits next to the $\\mathrm{C{=}O}$, not next to oxygen.","","The only structure consistent with all of this is $\\mathrm{CH_3CH_2{-}CO{-}O{-}CH_3}$."].join(`
`),marks:5},{question:"W has an isomer, ethyl ethanoate ($\\mathrm{CH_3COOCH_2CH_3}$). With reference to TWO spectroscopic techniques, explain why a chemist identifying W should not rely on a single technique.",sampleAnswer:["Each technique shows only certain features of a molecule, and isomers can look identical by one method while differing in another.","","**Infrared** would give almost the same spectrum for W and ethyl ethanoate: both have an ester $\\mathrm{C{=}O}$ near $1740\\ \\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$, a $\\mathrm{C{-}O}$ stretch and no $\\mathrm{O{-}H}$. IR confirms the functional group but cannot say how the carbon atoms are arranged around it. The molecular ion in a **mass spectrum** would also be $88$ for both, because they share the formula $\\mathrm{C_4H_8O_2}$.","","**$^1\\mathrm{H}$ NMR** separates them. Both show a $3:2:3$ pattern of singlet, quartet and triplet, but the chemical shifts differ. In W the singlet ($\\mathrm{O{-}CH_3}$) is near $3.7\\ \\mathrm{ppm}$ and the quartet is near $2.3\\ \\mathrm{ppm}$. In ethyl ethanoate the quartet ($\\mathrm{O{-}CH_2}$) is near $4.1\\ \\mathrm{ppm}$ and the singlet ($\\mathrm{CH_3{-}C{=}O}$) is near $2.0\\ \\mathrm{ppm}$.","","Using several techniques together lets each one cover the gaps of the others (formula and fragments from MS, functional groups from IR, connectivity from NMR). This makes the identification far more reliable than any single spectrum."].join(`
