`),sampleAnswer:["**Identifying the metal ion (flame test, plus a check for copper):**","","- Dip a clean nichrome wire loop into the solution and hold it in the hot (blue) part of a Bunsen flame.","- Intense yellow-orange flame → sodium ion.","- Lilac (pale purple) flame → potassium ion.","- Blue-green flame → copper(II) ion. A blue solution already hints at copper(II); confirm by adding dilute NaOH(aq) to a fresh sample: a pale blue precipitate forms.","","$$\\mathrm{Cu^{2+}(aq) + 2OH^-(aq) \\rightarrow Cu(OH)_2(s)}$$","","Note that if copper(II) is present the anion cannot be carbonate, because copper(II) carbonate is insoluble and the compound is known to dissolve.","","**Identifying the non-metal ion (the order matters):**","","1. Add dilute nitric acid to a fresh sample. Bubbles of a colourless gas (which turns limewater milky) show carbonate is present:","","$$\\mathrm{CO_3^{2-}(aq) + 2H^+(aq) \\rightarrow CO_2(g) + H_2O(l)}$$","","2. If there is no fizzing, add barium nitrate solution to a fresh acidified sample. A white precipitate shows sulfate:","","$$\\mathrm{Ba^{2+}(aq) + SO_4^{2-}(aq) \\rightarrow BaSO_4(s)}$$","","3. If there is still no precipitate, add silver nitrate solution to a fresh acidified sample. A white precipitate that darkens in light confirms chloride:","","$$\\mathrm{Ag^+(aq) + Cl^-(aq) \\rightarrow AgCl(s)}$$","","**Why this order?** Carbonate also forms white precipitates with both $\\mathrm{Ba^{2+}}$ and $\\mathrm{Ag^+}$, and silver sulfate is only slightly soluble. Testing for carbonate first and acidifying with nitric acid (not hydrochloric or sulfuric acid, which would add the very ions being tested for) prevents a false positive."].join(`
`),marks:5}}},RYe=["CH-M8-3"],NYe=["Interpreting industrial process flow charts","Role of catalysts in industrial processes","Recycling unreacted materials","Economic and environmental factors in plant design"],HYe={id:"chemistry/2020/q23",version:1,variants:1,syllabusTopics:[...RYe],learningTopics:NYe,generate(t){return{question:["The flow chart summarises an industrial process for the synthesis of ethane-1,2-diol.","","The process involves: ethene and oxygen fed to Reactor 1 ($200{-}300^\\circ\\mathrm{C}$, catalyst) producing ethylene oxide and unreacted gases; gases recycled via Separator 1; ethylene oxide and water fed to Reactor 2 ($50{-}70^\\circ\\mathrm{C}$, catalyst) producing liquid ethane-1,2-diol and liquid by-products separated in Separator 2; products transported to markets or for further processing.","","Explain THREE factors that may have been considered in the design of this industrial process. Make specific reference to the flow chart."].join(`
`),sampleAnswer:["**Factor 1 — Use of a catalyst:**","","Catalysts are used in both reactors (Reactor 1 and Reactor 2). A catalyst increases the rate of reaction at a lower temperature, making the process more energy-efficient and economically viable.","","**Factor 2 — Recycling of unreacted reagents:**","","After Separator 1, unreacted ethene and oxygen are recycled back to Reactor 1. This maximises the use of raw materials, increases the overall yield obtained from the feedstock, reduces waste and lowers costs. (Recycling does not change the atom economy, which is fixed by the balanced equation.)","","**Factor 3 — Access to markets:**","","The major product (ethane-1,2-diol) and the by-products are both transported to markets for sale. Selling the by-products instead of disposing of them earns extra income and reduces waste, making the process more economically and environmentally sustainable. This does not change the atom economy, which depends only on the balanced equation, but it means less of the output is wasted. The plant location would be chosen near transport networks to facilitate efficient product delivery."].join(`
`),marks:4}}},jYe=["CH-M7-4","CH-M7-5"],BYe=["Identifying ester functional groups","Transesterification to produce biodiesel","Complete and incomplete combustion","Energy density calculations for fuels","Advantages and disadvantages of bioethanol"],zYe={id:"chemistry/2020/q24",version:1,variants:1,syllabusTopics:[...jYe],learningTopics:BYe,generate(t){return{question:["A farming co-operative makes its own biodiesel from canola oil. The oil is a triglyceride: a glycerol backbone joined to three long fatty acid chains by three ester links.","","The oil is warmed with methanol ($\\mathrm{CH_3OH}$) and a small amount of KOH, which acts as a catalyst. Each triglyceride molecule is converted into three molecules of fatty acid methyl ester (the biodiesel) and one molecule of glycerol ($\\mathrm{C_3H_5(OH)_3}$)."].join(`
