`),marks:1},{question:["A typical biodiesel molecule is methyl oleate, $\\mathrm{C_{19}H_{36}O_2}$, and a typical heavy diesel molecule is icosane, $\\mathrm{C_{20}H_{42}}$.","","Using balanced equations, explain why an engine running on biodiesel tends to emit less soot than the same engine running on diesel."].join(`
`),sampleAnswer:["Soot (carbon particles) forms when there is not enough oxygen for every carbon atom to be converted into $\\mathrm{CO_2}$ — that is, during incomplete combustion.","","Complete combustion of each fuel:","","$$\\mathrm{C_{20}H_{42} + \\dfrac{61}{2}O_2 \\rightarrow 20CO_2 + 21H_2O}$$","","$$\\mathrm{C_{19}H_{36}O_2 + 27O_2 \\rightarrow 19CO_2 + 18H_2O}$$","","Icosane needs $30.5$ mol of $\\mathrm{O_2}$ per mole of fuel, about $1.53$ mol $\\mathrm{O_2}$ per carbon atom. Methyl oleate needs only $27$ mol, about $1.42$ mol $\\mathrm{O_2}$ per carbon atom, because its two oxygen atoms are already built into the molecule.","","In an engine, the air supply is limited. Because biodiesel needs less oxygen from the air for each carbon atom, a larger fraction of its carbon is fully oxidised to $\\mathrm{CO_2}$. Diesel is more likely to burn incompletely, for example:","","$$\\mathrm{C_{20}H_{42} + \\dfrac{21}{2}O_2 \\rightarrow 20C + 21H_2O}$$","","so it produces more soot."].join(`
`),marks:3},{question:["The co-operative compares the two fuels using this data.","","| Fuel | Energy content ($\\mathrm{MJ\\ kg^{-1}}$) | Density ($\\mathrm{kg\\ L^{-1}}$) |","| --- | --- | --- |","| Diesel | $45$ | $0.84$ |","| Biodiesel | $37$ | $0.88$ |","","On a return trip to the grain silo, a truck burns $80.0\\ \\mathrm{L}$ of diesel, which releases $3024\\ \\mathrm{MJ}$ of energy.","","Calculate the volume of biodiesel the truck would need to obtain the same amount of energy."].join(`
`),sampleAnswer:["The energy needed is the same, $3024\\ \\mathrm{MJ}$, so first find the mass of biodiesel that releases this energy, then convert mass to volume using the density of **biodiesel** (not diesel).","","$$m(\\text{biodiesel}) = \\dfrac{3024\\ \\mathrm{MJ}}{37\\ \\mathrm{MJ\\ kg^{-1}}} = 81.73\\ \\mathrm{kg}$$","","$$V(\\text{biodiesel}) = \\dfrac{81.73\\ \\mathrm{kg}}{0.88\\ \\mathrm{kg\\ L^{-1}}} = 92.9\\ \\mathrm{L}$$","","Check: biodiesel stores less energy per kilogram and is also denser, so more than $80\\ \\mathrm{L}$ is expected. About $93\\ \\mathrm{L}$ of biodiesel is needed."].join(`
`),marks:2},{question:"Ethanol made by fermenting plant material can also replace a fossil fuel such as petrol. Explain TWO benefits and TWO drawbacks of this use of ethanol.",sampleAnswer:["**Benefits:**","","1. **Renewable source.** Ethanol can be made from crops such as sugar cane or corn that are regrown every season, whereas petrol comes from crude oil, which is finite.","","2. **Lower net carbon dioxide emissions.** The $\\mathrm{CO_2}$ released when ethanol burns was taken from the air by photosynthesis while the crop grew:","","$$\\mathrm{6CO_2 + 6H_2O \\rightarrow C_6H_{12}O_6 + 6O_2}$$","","so the fuel is closer to carbon neutral than petrol, which releases carbon that was locked underground.","","**Drawbacks:**","","1. **Competition for land and food.** Large areas of farmland, water and fertiliser are needed, which can push up food prices or lead to clearing of native vegetation.","","2. **Energy input and lower energy content.** Growing, harvesting, fermenting and especially distilling the ethanol all use energy, often from fossil fuels, which reduces the carbon benefit. Ethanol also releases less energy per litre than petrol, so more fuel must be burned for the same distance."].join(`
`),marks:4}]}}},DYe=["CH-M6-3"],WYe=["Thermometric titration to locate the equivalence point","Graphing data and extrapolating lines of best fit","Mole ratios in polyprotic acid neutralisation","Calculating concentration from titration data"],UYe={id:"chemistry/2020/q25",version:1,variants:1,syllabusTopics:[...DYe],learningTopics:WYe,generate(t){return{question:["The concentration of a potassium hydroxide solution was found by a thermometric titration, using its reaction with sulfuric acid:","","$$\\mathrm{H_2SO_4(aq) + 2KOH(aq) \\rightarrow K_2SO_4(aq) + 2H_2O(l)}$$","","Eight polystyrene cups each held $10.0\\ \\mathrm{mL}$ of the KOH solution. A different volume of $0.50\\ \\mathrm{mol\\ L^{-1}}$ sulfuric acid was added to each cup, and each mixture was topped up with deionised water so that every cup contained $20.0\\ \\mathrm{mL}$ in total. The largest temperature rise in each cup was recorded.","","| Volume of $0.50\\ \\mathrm{mol\\ L^{-1}}$ $\\mathrm{H_2SO_4}$ (mL) | Temperature rise (°C) |","| --- | --- |","| 0.0 | 0.00 |","| 1.0 | 1.35 |","| 2.0 | 2.75 |","| 3.0 | 4.05 |","| 4.0 | 4.90 |","| 5.0 | 4.85 |","| 6.0 | 4.95 |","| 7.0 | 4.90 |","","Graph these results and use your graph to calculate the concentration of the potassium hydroxide solution."].join(`
`),sampleAnswer:["**Why the graph has two straight sections.** Every cup has the same total volume, so the temperature rise is proportional to the amount of neutralisation that happens.","","- While acid is the limiting reagent, each extra millilitre of acid neutralises more KOH, so the temperature rise increases steadily.","- Once all of the KOH has been used up, extra acid has nothing to react with, so the temperature rise stays constant.","","The equivalence point is where these two lines meet.","","**Drawing the graph.** Plot temperature rise (y-axis) against volume of acid (x-axis).","","- Rising line of best fit through $(0, 0)$, $(1.0, 1.35)$, $(2.0, 2.75)$ and $(3.0, 4.05)$: gradient $\\approx 1.35\\ ^\\circ\\mathrm{C\\ mL^{-1}}$.","- Horizontal line of best fit through the last four points: temperature rise $\\approx 4.90\\ ^\\circ\\mathrm{C}$.","","Do not simply pick the first point on the plateau ($4.0\\ \\mathrm{mL}$): at that volume the acid is already in excess.","","$$V_{\\text{equivalence}} \\approx \\dfrac{4.90}{1.35} \\approx 3.6\\ \\mathrm{mL}$$","","**Calculation.**","","$$n(\\mathrm{H_2SO_4}) = 0.50\\ \\mathrm{mol\\ L^{-1}} \\times 3.6 \\times 10^{-3}\\ \\mathrm{L} = 1.8 \\times 10^{-3}\\ \\mathrm{mol}$$","","Sulfuric acid is diprotic, so the mole ratio is $\\mathrm{H_2SO_4 : KOH} = 1 : 2$:","","$$n(\\mathrm{KOH}) = 2 \\times 1.8 \\times 10^{-3} = 3.6 \\times 10^{-3}\\ \\mathrm{mol}$$","","This KOH was in the $10.0\\ \\mathrm{mL}$ sample (the added water does not change the amount of KOH):","","$$c(\\mathrm{KOH}) = \\dfrac{3.6 \\times 10^{-3}\\ \\mathrm{mol}}{10.0 \\times 10^{-3}\\ \\mathrm{L}} = 0.36\\ \\mathrm{mol\\ L^{-1}}$$","","A graph read to within about $\\pm 0.1\\ \\mathrm{mL}$ gives an answer between about $0.35$ and $0.37\\ \\mathrm{mol\\ L^{-1}}$."].join(`
