`),parts:[{question:"Write a balanced equation, including states, for this equilibrium.",sampleAnswer:["$$\\mathrm{CO(g) + Cl_2(g) \\rightleftharpoons COCl_2(g)}$$","","One molecule of each reactant combines to give one product molecule, so no coefficients are needed. The double arrow shows the reaction is reversible."].join(`
`),marks:1},{question:["The energy profile diagram for the reaction is shown.",XYe(),"With reference to the diagram, use collision theory to explain the effect of raising the temperature on the value of $K_{\\mathrm{eq}}$ for this equilibrium."],sampleAnswer:["**Reading the diagram.** The products ($\\mathrm{COCl_2}$) are lower in energy than the reactants ($\\mathrm{CO + Cl_2}$), so the forward reaction is **exothermic** and the reverse reaction is endothermic. The peak of the curve is much further above the product level than above the reactant level, so the **reverse reaction has the larger activation energy**:","","$$E_\\mathrm{a}(\\text{reverse}) = E_\\mathrm{a}(\\text{forward}) + |\\Delta H|$$","","**Collision theory.** Raising the temperature increases the average kinetic energy of the molecules, so a larger fraction of collisions have at least the activation energy. Both rates increase. However, the fraction of molecules able to cross a *high* barrier grows proportionally much more than the fraction able to cross a low barrier. So the rate of the reverse reaction (larger $E_\\mathrm{a}$) increases by a greater factor than the rate of the forward reaction.","","**Effect on the equilibrium.** With the reverse reaction now faster than the forward reaction, $\\mathrm{COCl_2}$ is used up faster than it is made. The equilibrium position shifts to the **left** until the rates are equal again, so at the new equilibrium $[\\mathrm{COCl_2}]$ is lower and $[\\mathrm{CO}]$ and $[\\mathrm{Cl_2}]$ are higher.","","$$K_{\\mathrm{eq}} = \\dfrac{[\\mathrm{COCl_2}]}{[\\mathrm{CO}][\\mathrm{Cl_2}]}$$","","The numerator falls and the denominator rises, so **$K_{\\mathrm{eq}}$ decreases** at the higher temperature. This agrees with Le Chatelier’s principle: heating favours the endothermic (reverse) direction."].join(`
