`),marks:2},{question:'A student claims that because the acid was "exactly neutralised", the ammonium nitrate solution has a pH of 7. Evaluate this claim.',sampleAnswer:["The claim is **incorrect**: the ammonium nitrate solution is slightly **acidic** (pH below 7).","",'"Exactly neutralised" only means that equal amounts of acid and base have reacted. The pH of the resulting salt solution depends on how its ions react with water.',"","- $\\mathrm{NO_3^-}$ is the conjugate base of a **strong** acid. It has negligible tendency to accept protons, so it does not affect the pH.","- $\\mathrm{NH_4^+}$ is the conjugate acid of a **weak** base, ammonia. It donates some protons to water:","$$\\mathrm{NH_4^+(aq) + H_2O(l) \\rightleftharpoons NH_3(aq) + H_3O^+(aq)}$$","","This reaction produces $\\mathrm{H_3O^+}$, so $[\\mathrm{H_3O^+}] > [\\mathrm{OH^-}]$ and the pH is below 7. A pH of exactly 7 at the equivalence point is expected only when a strong acid reacts with a strong base."].join(`
