`),choices:a,sampleAnswer:{correctChoiceIndex:e,explanation:["The toxins have low solubility, so only a small amount can dissolve in the water around the seeds at any one time. This sets up a dissolution equilibrium:","$$\\text{toxin(s)} \\rightleftharpoons \\text{toxin(aq)}$$","Running water constantly carries the dissolved toxin away. Removing the product keeps shifting the equilibrium to the right, so more toxin dissolves. Over days or weeks almost all of it is removed. Still water would soon become saturated and dissolving would stop, which is why a flowing stream and a long soaking time were used.","","The other statements do not fit. The process removes the toxins physically; water does not react with them to make new substances. Keeping the food cool or making it softer to digest would not remove a poison."].join(`
