`),marks:2}}},_nt=["CH-M5-2","CH-M5-3"],Pnt=["Writing equilibrium constant expressions","Effect of temperature on $K_{eq}$","Le Chatelier's principle for temperature changes","Deducing whether a reaction is exothermic or endothermic from equilibrium shifts","Using colour changes to track equilibrium position"],lB=[{setup:["A sealed glass syringe contains a mixture of nitrogen dioxide and dinitrogen tetroxide at equilibrium.","","$$\\mathrm{2NO_2(g) \\rightleftharpoons N_2O_4(g)}$$","","$\\mathrm{NO_2}$ is brown and $\\mathrm{N_2O_4}$ is colourless. When the syringe is placed in hot water, the brown colour becomes darker."],answer:["A darker brown colour means $[\\mathrm{NO_2}]$ has increased, so heating has shifted the equilibrium to the **left** (towards the reactant).","","By Le Chatelier's principle, raising the temperature favours the endothermic direction, because it absorbs the added heat. The reverse reaction must therefore be endothermic, and the forward reaction is **exothermic**.","","$$K_{eq} = \\frac{[\\mathrm{N_2O_4}]}{[\\mathrm{NO_2}]^2}$$","","At the higher temperature the numerator is smaller and the denominator is larger, so $K_{eq}$ **decreases** as the temperature rises. Temperature is the only change that alters the value of $K_{eq}$; adding reactants or products would shift the position but leave $K_{eq}$ unchanged."]},{setup:["A test tube contains the following equilibrium mixture.","","$$\\mathrm{Fe^{3+}(aq) + SCN^-(aq) \\rightleftharpoons FeSCN^{2+}(aq)}$$","","$\\mathrm{Fe^{3+}}$ is pale yellow and $\\mathrm{FeSCN^{2+}}$ is deep red. When the test tube is placed in an ice bath, the solution becomes a deeper red."],answer:["A deeper red colour means $[\\mathrm{FeSCN^{2+}}]$ has increased, so cooling has shifted the equilibrium to the **right** (towards the product).","","By Le Chatelier's principle, lowering the temperature favours the exothermic direction, because it releases heat to replace some of the heat removed. The forward reaction is therefore **exothermic**.","","$$K_{eq} = \\frac{[\\mathrm{FeSCN^{2+}}]}{[\\mathrm{Fe^{3+}}][\\mathrm{SCN^-}]}$$","","At the lower temperature the numerator is larger and the denominator is smaller, so $K_{eq}$ **increases** as the temperature falls (and would decrease if the tube were heated). Temperature is the only change that alters the value of $K_{eq}$."]},{setup:["Copper(II) ions and chloride ions are in equilibrium in a beaker.","","$$\\mathrm{[Cu(H_2O)_6]^{2+}(aq) + 4Cl^-(aq) \\rightleftharpoons [CuCl_4]^{2-}(aq) + 6H_2O(l)}$$","","$\\mathrm{[Cu(H_2O)_6]^{2+}}$ is blue and $\\mathrm{[CuCl_4]^{2-}}$ is yellow-green. When the beaker is warmed on a hotplate, the solution changes from blue to green."],answer:["The change towards green means $[\\mathrm{[CuCl_4]^{2-}}]$ has increased, so heating has shifted the equilibrium to the **right** (towards the products).","","By Le Chatelier's principle, raising the temperature favours the endothermic direction, because it absorbs the added heat. The forward reaction is therefore **endothermic**.","","Water is the solvent, so it is left out of the expression:","$$K_{eq} = \\frac{[\\mathrm{[CuCl_4]^{2-}}]}{[\\mathrm{[Cu(H_2O)_6]^{2+}}][\\mathrm{Cl^-}]^4}$$","","At the higher temperature the numerator is larger and the denominator is smaller, so $K_{eq}$ **increases** as the temperature rises. Temperature is the only change that alters the value of $K_{eq}$."]}],Ent={id:"chemistry/2024/q23",version:1,variants:lB.length,syllabusTopics:[..._nt],learningTopics:Pnt,generate(t){const e=lB[t];return{question:[...e.setup,"","Explain what this observation shows about the enthalpy change of the forward reaction and about how the value of $K_{eq}$ changes."].join(`
