`)}}}},prt=["DT-IET-4"],frt=["ethical and legal responsibilities in design","privacy, security, and responsible decision-making"],grt={id:"design_and_technology/2016/q06",version:1,variants:4,syllabusTopics:[...prt],learningTopics:frt,generate(t){const e=[2,3,0,1][t],n="Enables the consumer to enjoy the product and know it will minimise harm",i=["Ensures that entrepreneurs invest in their designs","Enables the user to purchase goods for their intended use","Uses technologically advanced production processes and renewable resources"],a=[];let o=0;for(let s=0;s<4;s++)a.push(s===e?n:i[o++]);return{question:"A design company acts ethically when it",choices:a,sampleAnswer:{correctChoiceIndex:e,explanation:["A design company acts ethically when it enables the consumer to enjoy the product","and know it will minimise harm.","","Ethical design prioritises the wellbeing and safety of consumers. This means","producing products that are safe to use, do not cause harm to users or the environment,","and deliver genuine value. Transparency about potential risks is also part of","ethical responsibility.","","Attracting investment, enabling product purchases, and using renewable resources","are related but do not capture the essential ethical principle of ensuring consumer","safety and harm minimisation."].join(`
`)}}}},$rt=["DT-DP-5"],yrt=["Collaborative design practice","Teamwork and sharing ideas in design","Comparing collaborative and hierarchical design approaches"],brt={id:"design_and_technology/2016/q07",version:1,variants:4,syllabusTopics:[...$rt],learningTopics:yrt,generate(t){const e=[3,0,1,2][t],n="Designers, engineers and users meet regularly to exchange and build on one another’s ideas",i=["Each designer juggles several unrelated projects at the same time","The senior designer settles the concept and hands tasks down to the team","The whole tent project is outsourced to one external consultant"],a=[];let o=0;for(let s=0;s<4;s++)a.push(s===e?n:i[o++]);return{question:["A studio that makes outdoor camping gear will use collaborative design to","develop its next lightweight tent.","","Which statement best describes how the team is likely to work?"].join(`
`),choices:a,sampleAnswer:{correctChoiceIndex:e,explanation:["Collaborative design means people with different expertise develop the design","together. Ideas flow in all directions and are refined through discussion,","rather than being decided by one person.","","For a tent, designers, fabric engineers and experienced campers meeting to","exchange ideas and build on each other’s suggestions is the clearest example.","Each person notices problems the others might miss, such as weight, pitching","time or wind resistance.","","Why the others are not collaboration:","- Juggling several projects is about individual workload, not shared idea","  development.","- A senior designer deciding and handing down tasks is a top-down (hierarchical)","  approach, the opposite of collaboration.","- Outsourcing to one consultant puts the design in a single person’s hands."].join(`
`)}}}},wrt=["DT-DP-4"],vrt=["Life cycle analysis of a product","Environmental impacts at each stage of a product's life","Principles of sustainable design"],xrt={id:"design_and_technology/2016/q08",version:1,variants:4,syllabusTopics:[...wrt],learningTopics:vrt,generate(t){const e=[2,3,0,1][t],n="The environmental effects of the bottle from raw materials through manufacture, use and disposal",i=["How many years the bottle will stay popular before its sales start to fall","Whether the finished bottle meets Australian safety standards","How the bottle's retail price compares with rival bottles in shops"],a=[];let o=0;for(let s=0;s<4;s++)a.push(s===e?n:i[o++]);return{question:["A kitchenware company pays a consultant to carry out a life cycle analysis (LCA)","of its stainless-steel drink bottle.","","What information will the LCA mainly give the company?"].join(`
`),choices:a,sampleAnswer:{correctChoiceIndex:e,explanation:['A life cycle analysis follows a product "from cradle to grave" and measures its',"environmental impact at every stage: mining and refining the steel, forming and","finishing the bottle, transporting it, washing it during use, and finally","recycling or dumping it. The company can then see which stage causes the most","harm and redesign to reduce it.","","Why the others are wrong:",'- How long the bottle stays popular is the marketing "product life cycle"',"  (introduction, growth, maturity, decline). It has a similar name but is not an","  environmental analysis. This is the most common trap.","- Checking safety standards is compliance testing, not LCA.","- Comparing retail prices is a market or economic comparison."].join(`
`)}}}},krt=["DT-DP-3","DT-DP-13"],Trt=["Planned (built-in) obsolescence","Commercial motivations behind design decisions","Environmental consequences of short product lifespans","Ethical responsibilities of designers and manufacturers"],Art={id:"design_and_technology/2016/q09",version:1,variants:4,syllabusTopics:[...krt],learningTopics:Trt,generate(t){const e=[0,1,2,3][t],n="To make customers buy replacement printers more often",i=["To reduce the amount of electronic waste sent to landfill","To extend the printer's working life well past its warranty","To meet Australian electrical safety standards"],a=[];let o=0;for(let s=0;s<4;s++)a.push(s===e?n:i[o++]);return{question:["A manufacturer fits its home printers with a counter chip that shuts the printer","down permanently after 15 000 pages, even though the mechanism is still in good","working order.","","What is the most likely reason the company designed the printer this way?"].join(`
`),choices:a,sampleAnswer:{correctChoiceIndex:e,explanation:["Deliberately ending a product’s life while it still works is called planned, or","built-in, obsolescence. The motive is commercial: a printer that stops after","15 000 pages forces the owner back into the shop, giving the company a steady","stream of repeat sales.","","Why the others are wrong:","- Shutting down working printers creates more electronic waste, not less.","- A built-in shutdown shortens the working life; it cannot extend it.","- A page counter has nothing to do with electrical safety, since the mechanism","  is described as sound.","","Planned obsolescence raises ethical and environmental concerns, which is why","repairable, longer-lasting design is encouraged as the sustainable alternative."].join(`
`)}}}},Crt=["DT-DP-4"],Srt=["Principles of sustainable design","Minimising resource and energy use","Environmentally friendly materials and processes","Life cycle thinking in design"],qrt={id:"design_and_technology/2016/q10",version:1,variants:4,syllabusTopics:[...Crt],learningTopics:Srt,generate(t){const e=[3,0,1,2][t],n="Use renewable or recycled materials and minimise the resources and energy used over the toys' whole life",i=["Base the range on whichever cartoon characters are currently most popular","Make the toys tough enough to outlast every competing brand in the shop","Cut production costs so the toys can be sold at a lower price"],a=[];let o=0;for(let s=0;s<4;s++)a.push(s===e?n:i[o++]);return{question:["A designer has been asked to make a new range of children's wooden toys as","sustainable as possible.","","Which approach would best achieve this goal?"].join(`
`),choices:a,sampleAnswer:{correctChoiceIndex:e,explanation:["Sustainable design meets people’s needs today without using up resources or","damaging the environment for future generations. It looks at the whole life of","the product, not just one stage.","","Choosing renewable timber or recycled materials and cutting the materials and","energy used in making, shipping and finally disposing of the toys addresses","sustainability directly across that whole life.","","Why the others fall short:","- Following popular characters is a marketing choice; it may even shorten how","  long children want the toys.","- Toughness helps a toy last, but beating rival brands is a commercial aim, and","  durability alone ignores materials, energy and disposal.","- Lower production cost is an economic goal and can mean cheaper, less","  sustainable materials."].join(`
`)}}}},Irt=["DT-DP-3","DT-DP-6"],_rt=["Factors affecting design and production","Ergonomics and anthropometrics in furniture design","Function and intended use of a product","Selecting materials to suit purpose","Aesthetics as a factor affecting design"],Prt={id:"design_and_technology/2016/q11",version:1,variants:1,syllabusTopics:[...Irt],learningTopics:_rt,generate(t){return{question:["Two chairs have been developed for different uses: an **office chair** (a padded,","adjustable chair on castors designed for extended desk work) and a **kitchen bench stool**","(a tall, minimalist steel stool designed for short-term seating at a kitchen bench).","","How have design factors been applied differently in the development of the two chairs?","Support your answer with examples."].join(`
`),sampleAnswer:["The design factors of ergonomics, function, aesthetics, and materials have been applied","differently in each chair to suit their intended use.","","**Ergonomics and function:** The office chair is designed for extended periods of sitting,","so ergonomic factors such as lumbar back support, height adjustment, and armrests have","been incorporated to reduce fatigue and support the user's posture over many hours.","Castors allow the user to move around without leaving the seat. In contrast, the kitchen","bench stool is designed for short-term use at a raised surface. It does not require the","same degree of postural support, but its increased height is critical to ergonomic use","at a bench — a different ergonomic priority than the office chair.","","**Aesthetics and materials:** The office chair uses padded upholstery and mesh to create","a professional appearance appropriate for a workplace environment. The kitchen bench stool","uses steel and a simpler aesthetic suited to a domestic kitchen setting, where the stool","may also need to withstand spills and be easy to clean. The material choices reflect the","different functional and aesthetic demands of each environment."].join(`
`),marks:4}}},Ert=["DT-DP-12","DT-DP-8"],Ort=["Purpose of ongoing evaluation in the design process","Evaluating against criteria for success","Using technology to gather and analyse feedback","Testing, simulation and user trials"],Lrt={id:"design_and_technology/2016/q12",version:1,variants:1,syllabusTopics:[...Ert],learningTopics:Ort,generate(t){return{question:[],parts:[{question:"Why is ongoing evaluation important to a designer?",sampleAnswer:["Ongoing evaluation is important because it allows the designer to continually","monitor whether the design is meeting the original brief and the needs of the user","throughout the entire design process, not just at the end.","","By gathering feedback at each stage, the designer can identify problems early and","make adjustments before they become costly to fix. This ensures the final product","is safe, functional, and aligned with the client's requirements."].join(`
`),marks:2},{question:"Discuss the use of technology to conduct an evaluation of a product.",sampleAnswer:["Technology provides a variety of powerful tools for evaluating a product, offering","both advantages and some limitations.","","Online surveys and social media platforms allow designers to reach large numbers of","consumers quickly and cost-effectively. Feedback can be collected in real time and","data can be automatically collated, analysed, and visualised, making it easy to","identify patterns in qualitative and quantitative responses.","","Computer-aided design (CAD) software enables virtual testing and simulation before","a physical prototype is built, identifying structural weaknesses or design flaws","early in the process without the cost of manufacturing a prototype.","","However, reliance on technology for evaluation may exclude users who are not","digitally literate or who lack internet access, creating a potentially unrepresentative","sample. Additionally, online responses may not fully capture the tactile and","experiential aspects of using a physical product.","","Overall, technology enables faster, broader, and more cost-effective evaluation,","though it should be complemented with direct user testing to gain a complete picture."].join(`
`),marks:4}]}}},Mrt=["DT-IET-6"],Frt=["Impact of innovation on society","Innovative products that improve quality of life","Innovation for health, accessibility and independence"],Rrt={id:"design_and_technology/2016/q13",version:1,variants:1,syllabusTopics:[...Mrt],learningTopics:Frt,generate(t){return{question:"Explain how the development of innovative products may contribute to the quality of life.",sampleAnswer:["Innovative products may contribute to quality of life when they solve problems that","people experience in everyday life and allow them to live more safely, independently,","and efficiently.","","One way this happens is through improved health and mobility. For example, 3D-printed","prosthetic limbs can be customised for the individual user, making them more comfortable","and functional. This can restore independence and allow a person to participate more","fully in work, school, and daily activities.","","Innovative products can also save time and reduce effort. GPS navigation, smart home","systems, and wearable devices help people make decisions more quickly, manage their","environment, and monitor their wellbeing in real time. This improves convenience and","can reduce stress.","","A further contribution is improved safety and access. Products that warn users about","health issues, assist people with disability, or make communication easier can increase","inclusion and confidence for a wider range of users.","","Therefore, innovative products improve quality of life not just because they are new,","but because they help people do important tasks better and with greater comfort, safety,","and independence."].join(`
