`),choices:["AUD 1100 million","AUD 1025 million","AUD 1075 million","AUD 900 million"],correctChoiceIndex:0,explanation:["Step 1: find the marginal propensity to consume (MPC), the share of each extra dollar of income that is spent.","","$$MPC = \\dfrac{\\Delta C}{\\Delta Y} = \\dfrac{770 - 620}{1000 - 800} = \\dfrac{150}{200} = 0.75$$","","Step 2: find the multiplier.","","$$k = \\dfrac{1}{1 - MPC} = \\dfrac{1}{0.25} = 4$$","","Step 3: the extra investment is an injection, and each round of spending creates further income. The total change in income is $4 \\times 25 = 100$ million, so Year 3 income is $1000 + 100 = 1100$ million.","","Common traps: AUD 1025 million adds the extra investment with no multiplier effect. AUD 1075 million counts only the later rounds of consumption spending (a multiplier of 3) and forgets the initial AUD 25 million of investment. AUD 900 million treats a rise in investment as if it were a fall."].join(`
`)})},{variants:he({question:["The table shows export and import price indices for a commodity-exporting economy.","","| Year | Export price index | Import price index |","| --- | ---: | ---: |","| 1 | 100 | 100 |","| 2 | 120 | 110 |","| 3 | 108 | 112 |","","Which option shows how the terms of trade (ToT) changed between Year 2 and Year 3, together with a possible cause?"].join(`
