`)})},{variants:he({question:[...pD,"Assuming nothing else changes, how does the tariff cut affect the government’s tariff revenue from this good?"],choices:["Decrease of AUD 120 million","Increase of AUD 120 million","Decrease of AUD 200 million","Increase of AUD 180 million"],correctChoiceIndex:0,explanation:["Tariff revenue = tariff per unit × quantity imported, where imports = domestic demand − domestic supply at the tariff-inclusive price.","","Before the cut: the domestic price is 8 + 6 = AUD 14. The diagram shows demand of 90 million and domestic supply of 40 million, so imports are 50 million units. Revenue = 6 × 50 million = AUD 300 million.","","After the cut: the domestic price is 8 + 2 = AUD 10. Demand rises to 110 million and domestic supply falls to 20 million, so imports are 90 million units. Revenue = 2 × 90 million = AUD 180 million.","","Revenue falls from AUD 300 million to AUD 180 million, a decrease of AUD 120 million. Imports rise sharply, but not by enough to make up for the much smaller tariff per unit.","","Common traps: an increase of AUD 180 million treats the new revenue level as the change. A decrease of AUD 200 million multiplies the AUD 4 cut in the tariff by the original 50 million imports and ignores the extra 40 million units now imported. Expecting revenue to rise because imports rise overlooks the fall in the tariff collected on each unit."].join(`
`)})},{variants:he({question:[...pD,"Assuming nothing else changes, how does the tariff cut change the revenue that foreign producers earn from selling this good in the economy?"],choices:["Increase of AUD 320 million","Increase of AUD 200 million","Increase of AUD 720 million","Increase of AUD 400 million"],correctChoiceIndex:0,explanation:["Foreign producers receive only the world price for each unit they sell. The tariff added on top is collected by the government, so it is not part of their revenue.","","Before the cut: the domestic price is 8 + 6 = AUD 14. Demand is 90 million and domestic supply is 40 million, so imports are 50 million units. Foreign revenue = 8 × 50 million = AUD 400 million.","","After the cut: the domestic price is 8 + 2 = AUD 10. Demand is 110 million and domestic supply is 20 million, so imports are 90 million units. Foreign revenue = 8 × 90 million = AUD 720 million.","","Foreign producers gain AUD 720 million − AUD 400 million = AUD 320 million, because they sell 40 million more units at the unchanged world price.","","Common traps: AUD 720 million and AUD 400 million are the revenue levels after and before the cut, not the change. AUD 200 million values imports at the domestic price (10 × 90 − 14 × 50 = 900 − 700), wrongly counting the tariff as income for foreign producers."].join(`
