`),choices:["The AUD rises 8% against the euro and falls 8% against the US dollar","The AUD rises 8% against the yen and falls 8% against the rupee","The AUD rises 8% against the rupee and falls 8% against the renminbi","The AUD rises 8% against the euro and falls 8% against the yen"],correctChoiceIndex:1,explanation:["The TWI measures the value of the AUD against a basket of currencies, with each currency weighted by that country's share of Australia's trade. When the AUD moves by the same percentage against two currencies in opposite directions, the movement against the currency with the larger weight decides which way the TWI goes.","","For the TWI to rise, the AUD must appreciate against the more heavily weighted currency. Rising 8% against the yen (weight 9%) and falling 8% against the rupee (weight 4%) gives a net effect of about $8 \\times (0.09 - 0.04) = 0.4$ per cent, so the TWI rises.","","In every other pair the fall is against the currency with the larger weight, so the TWI falls: the US dollar (11%) outweighs the euro (8%), the renminbi (28%) outweighs the rupee (4%), and the yen (9%) only just outweighs the euro (8%). The last pair is the closest trap, because the two weights differ by only one percentage point."].join(`
`)})}],q5t=["EC-H1"],I5t=["Role of the OECD in the global economy","Roles of the IMF, World Bank, WTO and G20","Distinguishing functions of international economic institutions"],_5t=ue("economics/2023/q01",Di[0].variants,I5t,q5t),P5t=["EC-H2"],E5t=["Composition of Australia's exports","Importance of mining and resource exports to Australia","Comparing rural, manufacturing and services exports"],O5t=ue("economics/2023/q02",Di[1].variants,E5t,P5t),L5t=["EC-H3"],M5t=["Calculating and interpreting the participation rate","Effect of population ageing on labour force participation","Effect of childcare, pension age and job vacancies on labour supply"],F5t=ue("economics/2023/q03",Di[2].variants,M5t,L5t),R5t=["EC-H3"],N5t=["Components of aggregate demand","Causes of shifts in the AD curve","Effect of household saving and confidence on consumption","Distinguishing AD shifts from AS shifts"],H5t=ue("economics/2023/q04",Di[3].variants,N5t,R5t),j5t=["EC-H1","EC-H2"],B5t=["Effects of a tariff on domestic prices and consumers","Effects of protection on exporting countries","Winners and losers from trade protection"],z5t=ue("economics/2023/q05",Di[4].variants,B5t,j5t),D5t=["EC-H1","EC-H2"],W5t=["Multilateral versus bilateral free trade agreements","Long-run gains from trade liberalisation","Trade creation and trade diversion","Structural unemployment from trade liberalisation"],U5t=ue("economics/2023/q06",Di[5].variants,W5t,D5t),Y5t=["EC-P3"],G5t=["Characteristics of public goods","Non-rivalry and non-excludability","Distinguishing public goods from government-provided goods"],V5t=ue("economics/2023/q07",Di[6].variants,G5t,Y5t),X5t=["EC-H4"],K5t=["Budget outcomes: surplus, deficit and balance","Identifying expansionary and contractionary fiscal stance","Interpreting changes in the budget outcome"],Q5t=ue("economics/2023/q08",Di[7].variants,K5t,X5t),J5t=["EC-H1"],Z5t=["Components of the human development index","Economic growth versus economic development","Effects of trade openness, foreign investment and technology on development"],e4t=ue("economics/2023/q09",Di[8].variants,Z5t,J5t),t4t=["EC-H1"],n4t=["How import quotas work","Arguments for protection, including anti-dumping"],i4t=ue("economics/2023/q10",Di[9].variants,n4t,t4t),a4t=["EC-H2"],o4t=["Demand for and supply of the AUD in the FOREX market","Factors that shift the supply of AUD","Effect of import demand on the exchange rate"],s4t=ue("economics/2023/q11",Di[10].variants,o4t,a4t),r4t=["EC-H4"],l4t=["Operation of the cash rate and interest rate corridor","Transmission mechanism of monetary policy","Effect of lower interest rates on asset prices","Effect of lower interest rates on investment"],c4t=ue("economics/2023/q12",Di[11].variants,l4t,r4t),h4t=["EC-H2"],d4t=["Net foreign liabilities: net foreign debt plus net foreign equity","Calculating net foreign debt and net foreign equity","Distinguishing debt from equity liabilities"],u4t=ue("economics/2023/q13",Di[12].variants,d4t,h4t),m4t=["EC-H3","EC-P3"],p4t=["Negative externalities of production","Marginal private cost versus marginal social cost","Using taxes to correct market failure"],f4t=ue("economics/2023/q14",Di[13].variants,p4t,m4t),g4t=["EC-H2"],$4t=["Calculating the terms of trade index","Effect of export and import price changes on the terms of trade","Interpreting a deterioration in the terms of trade"],y4t=ue("economics/2023/q15",Di[14].variants,$4t,g4t),b4t=["EC-H2"],w4t=["Fixed exchange rate systems","Central bank intervention in the FOREX market","Excess demand or supply of currency at a fixed rate"],v4t=ue("economics/2023/q16",Di[15].variants,w4t,b4t),x4t=["EC-H3"],k4t=["Construction of the consumer price index","Weighted contribution of CPI expenditure groups to inflation","Measuring inflation from price indices"],T4t=ue("economics/2023/q17",Di[16].variants,k4t,x4t),A4t=["EC-H3"],C4t=["Marginal propensity to consume and save","Calculating the multiplier","Effect of government spending on national income"],S4t=ue("economics/2023/q18",Di[17].variants,C4t,A4t),q4t=["EC-H1","EC-H2","EC-P3"],I4t=["Domestic demand and supply at the world price","Measuring imports from a trade diagram","Effect of a production subsidy on domestic supply","Calculating the subsidy needed to reduce imports"],_4t=ue("economics/2023/q19",Di[18].variants,I4t,q4t),P4t=["EC-H2"],E4t=["Construction of the Trade Weighted Index","Effect of bilateral exchange rate changes on the TWI","Appreciation and depreciation of the AUD"],O4t=ue("economics/2023/q20",Di[19].variants,E4t,P4t),L4t=["EC-H1"],M4t=["Functions of the World Trade Organization","Opportunity cost and comparative advantage","Specialisation and the gains from trade","Agricultural protection in high-income economies","Trade liberalisation and export-led growth in developing economies","Global inequality between and within countries"],F4t={id:"economics/2023/q21",version:1,variants:1,syllabusTopics:[...L4t],learningTopics:M4t,generate(t){return{question:[],parts:[{question:"Outline TWO ways in which the World Trade Organization (WTO) supports the global trading system.",sampleAnswer:["**Setting and enforcing trade rules.** The WTO provides a forum where member countries negotiate agreements that lower tariffs and other barriers, and it oversees rules such as non-discrimination (most-favoured-nation treatment), so that trade is more open and predictable.","","**Settling trade disputes.** When one member believes another has broken WTO rules, for example by imposing an unjustified tariff, it can take the case to the WTO dispute settlement process. A panel rules on the case, and if the ruling is ignored the WTO can authorise the complaining member to retaliate, which gives smaller economies a rules-based alternative to trade wars."].join(`
`),marks:2},{question:["The table shows the daily output of one worker in two hypothetical countries.","","| Country | Rice (tonnes per day) | Machine parts (units per day) |","| --- | --- | --- |","| Veloria | 8 | 4 |","| Kestrel | 6 | 1 |","","Using the table, explain why both countries can benefit from specialising and trading with each other."].join(`
`),sampleAnswer:["Veloria has an **absolute advantage** in both goods because its workers produce more of each. Trade still benefits both countries because what matters is **comparative advantage**, which depends on opportunity cost.","","| Country | Opportunity cost of 1 machine part | Opportunity cost of 1 tonne of rice |","| --- | --- | --- |","| Veloria | $8 \\div 4 = 2$ tonnes of rice | $4 \\div 8 = 0.5$ machine parts |","| Kestrel | $6 \\div 1 = 6$ tonnes of rice | $1 \\div 6 \\approx 0.17$ machine parts |","","Veloria gives up less rice to make a machine part (2 tonnes compared with 6), so it has a comparative advantage in machine parts. Kestrel gives up fewer machine parts to grow a tonne of rice (0.17 compared with 0.5), so it has a comparative advantage in rice.","","If Veloria specialises in machine parts and Kestrel in rice, total world output of the two goods rises. They can then trade at any rate between 2 and 6 tonnes of rice per machine part. For example, at 4 tonnes of rice per part, Veloria receives 4 tonnes for each part it exports, rather than the 2 tonnes it would get by switching a worker to rice. Kestrel pays 4 tonnes of rice for each part, rather than the 6 tonnes it would sacrifice to make the part itself. Both countries can therefore consume beyond what they could produce on their own."].join(`
`),marks:3},{question:"Several high-income economies agree to phase out their tariffs and farm subsidies on imported agricultural products. Explain how this reduction in trade protection could affect the global distribution of income.",sampleAnswer:["Tariffs and subsidies on farm products in high-income economies raise the price that low-cost foreign producers must charge and allow domestic farmers to sell below their true cost. Many developing economies have a comparative advantage in agriculture, so this protection shuts them out of the markets where they could earn the most.","","**Effects that could make income more equal between countries**","","- Farmers in developing economies gain access to large, high-price markets, so export volumes and prices rise. Higher export earnings lift incomes in rural areas, where much of the world’s extreme poverty is concentrated.","- Extra foreign exchange lets these economies import capital goods and technology, supporting further growth and structural change.","- Removing subsidies also stops surplus farm output being dumped on world markets, which had depressed the prices received by poor producers.","","**Effects that could limit or reverse the improvement**","","- The gains go mainly to countries that already have the infrastructure, quality standards and scale to export. The least developed economies, and net food importers, may gain little or face higher food import prices once subsidised supplies disappear.","- Within developing countries, large agribusinesses and multinational traders may capture most of the extra revenue, so inequality within these countries could widen even as the gap between countries narrows.","- Farmers in the high-income economies lose income and may need adjustment assistance, although this has little effect on the global distribution because they start from a much higher income level.","","On balance, lower agricultural protection is likely to narrow the income gap between high-income and developing economies, but the size of the change depends on whether poorer countries have the capacity to expand exports and on how the gains are shared within them."].join(`
`),marks:5}]}}},R4t=["EC-H2"],N4t=["Components of the current account","Balance of payments identity","Capital and financial account inflows financing a current account deficit","Net primary income and servicing foreign liabilities","Exchange rate depreciation and international competitiveness","J-curve effect","Valuation effects of depreciation on foreign debt"],H4t={id:"economics/2023/q22",version:1,variants:1,syllabusTopics:[...R4t],learningTopics:N4t,generate(t){return{question:["The table shows balance of payments data for one year in a hypothetical economy that has a floating exchange rate. Net errors and omissions are zero.","","| Item | \\$ billion |","| --- | --- |","| Exports of goods | 185 |","| Imports of goods | 172 |","| Net services | −9 |","| Net primary income | −38 |","| Net secondary income | ? |","| Capital account | −1 |","| Financial account | 43 |"].join(`
`),parts:[{question:"Calculate net secondary income for this economy, in \\$ billion.",sampleAnswer:["Under a floating exchange rate the balance of payments always sums to zero:","","$$\\text{Current account} + \\text{Capital account} + \\text{Financial account} = 0$$","","So the current account balance is","","$$\\text{CAB} = -(-1 + 43) = -42$$","","The current account is the sum of its components. Let $x$ be net secondary income:","","$$(185 - 172) + (-9) + (-38) + x = -42$$","","$$-34 + x = -42 \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad x = -8$$","","Net secondary income is **−\\$8 billion**. A common error is to leave out the capital account, which gives −\\$9 billion."].join(`
`),marks:1},{question:"Explain the relationship between the current account and the capital and financial account for this economy.",sampleAnswer:["This economy has a current account deficit of \\$42 billion: it pays more to the rest of the world for goods, services, income and transfers than it receives. The deficit is driven mainly by the net primary income deficit of \\$38 billion, the cost of servicing foreign debt and paying returns on foreign-owned investment.","","Because the exchange rate floats, the balance of payments must balance. Every dollar of the current account deficit has to be financed by a matching net inflow on the capital and financial account. Here the financial account surplus of \\$43 billion, less the capital account deficit of \\$1 billion, gives a net inflow of \\$42 billion, exactly offsetting the current account.","","In practice the inflow is borrowing from overseas lenders and the sale of domestic assets to foreign investors. This adds to foreign liabilities, which increases future primary income payments and so tends to keep the current account in deficit."].join(`
`),marks:3},{question:"Assess the likely impact of a depreciation of the exchange rate on the current account balance of this economy.",sampleAnswer:["A depreciation lowers the foreign-currency price of this economy’s exports and raises the domestic-currency price of its imports. Its effect on the \\$42 billion current account deficit depends on the time period and on the structure of the account.","","**Balance on goods and services.** Over time the depreciation should improve the goods and services balance, currently \\$4 billion in deficit (a \\$13 billion goods surplus and a \\$9 billion services deficit). Exporters become more price-competitive, so export volumes rise, while dearer imports encourage households and firms to switch to local substitutes. Services such as tourism and education, which are sensitive to price, are likely to respond strongly.","","**The J-curve.** In the short run the balance may get worse. Import and export contracts are often already set, and demand takes time to adjust, so the economy pays more for roughly the same volume of imports before volumes change. Only after this lag does the trade balance improve, tracing out a J shape.","","**Net primary income.** The largest item in the deficit is the \\$38 billion net primary income deficit. If a large part of the foreign debt is denominated in foreign currency, a depreciation raises the domestic-currency cost of interest payments and increases the value of the debt itself. This valuation effect could widen the primary income deficit and offset much of the improvement in trade.","","**Other limits.** The trade response is smaller if export capacity is fully used, if demand for imports is inelastic (for example imported fuel or capital equipment), or if higher import prices feed into domestic inflation and erode the competitiveness gain.","","**Judgement.** A depreciation is likely to reduce the current account deficit only modestly and only after a lag. The goods and services balance should improve in the medium term, but because this economy’s deficit is dominated by income payments on foreign liabilities, any rise in debt-servicing costs could leave the overall current account little changed."].join(`
`),marks:6}]}}},j4t=["EC-H3"],B4t=["Income quintile shares and cumulative income","Interpreting Lorenz curves","Progressive taxation and transfer payments","Household composition as a source of income inequality","Economic costs of income inequality","Social costs of income inequality"],z4t={id:"economics/2023/q23",version:1,variants:1,syllabusTopics:[...j4t],learningTopics:B4t,generate(t){return{question:["The table shows the share of total household income received by each fifth (quintile) of households in a hypothetical economy, ranked from lowest to highest income.","","| Household quintile | Share of income in 2015 (%) | Share of income in 2025 (%) |","| --- | --- | --- |","| Lowest 20% | 5 | 7 |","| Second 20% | 11 | 12 |","| Middle 20% | 16 | 17 |","| Fourth 20% | 23 | 23 |","| Highest 20% | 45 | 41 |"].join(`
`),parts:[{question:"Using the table, state whether the 2025 Lorenz curve lies closer to or further from the line of equality than the 2015 curve.",sampleAnswer:["Add the shares to find the cumulative income at each point on the Lorenz curve:","","| Cumulative households | 20% | 40% | 60% | 80% | 100% |","| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |","| Cumulative income 2015 (%) | 5 | 16 | 32 | 55 | 100 |","| Cumulative income 2025 (%) | 7 | 19 | 36 | 59 | 100 |","","At every quintile the 2025 cumulative share is higher, so the 2025 Lorenz curve lies **closer to the line of equality**. Income became more evenly distributed and the Gini coefficient fell."].join(`
`),marks:1},{question:"Outline ONE government policy that could have contributed to the change in income shares between 2015 and 2025.",sampleAnswer:["The data show the lowest quintile’s share rising from 5% to 7% while the highest quintile’s share fell from 45% to 41%.","","One policy that could produce this is a **more progressive tax and transfer system**. If the government raised the top marginal income tax rate and used the revenue to increase income support payments (such as unemployment and family payments), the disposable income of high earners would fall relative to low earners. Income is shifted from the top of the distribution to the bottom, lifting the lowest quintile’s share and reducing the Gini coefficient."].join(`
`),marks:2},{question:"Explain how differences in household composition can contribute to income inequality.",sampleAnswer:["Household income depends on how many members earn an income and how many depend on it. A household with two adults in paid work usually has a much higher income than a household with one adult, even when each worker earns the same wage.","","Single-parent households are often limited to one earner, and caring responsibilities may restrict that parent to part-time work. Households headed by retirees rely mostly on pensions or savings. These households are therefore over-represented in the lowest income quintiles, while dual-income couples without children are over-represented at the top. As the mix of household types changes, for example with more single-person and single-parent households, measured income inequality can widen."].join(`
`),marks:2},{question:"Suppose the trend later reverses and the highest quintile’s share rises back above 45%. Analyse the economic and social costs of this increase in income inequality.",sampleAnswer:["A rise in the top quintile’s share means a larger portion of income goes to households that are already well off, while lower and middle quintiles fall behind.","","**Economic costs**","","- **Weaker consumption.** Low-income households spend a larger proportion of each extra dollar (a higher marginal propensity to consume) than high-income households, which save more. Shifting income to the top can reduce aggregate demand and slow economic growth.","- **Wasted human capital.** Children in low-income households have less access to quality education, health care and housing. Their potential productivity is not developed, which lowers the economy’s long-run growth rate.","- **Higher government spending.** More households need welfare, public housing and health services, which increases pressure on the budget and may require higher taxes elsewhere.","","**Social costs**","","- **Lower social mobility.** When the gap between quintiles is wider, it is harder for children born into low-income households to move up, so disadvantage becomes entrenched across generations.","- **Poorer health and wellbeing.** Lower-income groups experience higher rates of chronic illness, housing stress and financial stress.","- **Weaker social cohesion.** Large income gaps can increase crime, reduce trust in institutions and produce geographic concentrations of disadvantage.","","These costs reinforce each other: poorer health and education reduce workforce participation and productivity, which further lowers incomes at the bottom of the distribution. Some inequality provides incentives to work, train and invest, but a sustained rise of this size is likely to impose economic and social costs that outweigh those incentive benefits."].join(`
`),marks:5}]}}},D4t=["EC-H4","EC-H3","EC-P4"],W4t=["Structural unemployment and skills mismatch","Labour market programs: training and job-search assistance","Methods of wage determination: awards, enterprise agreements and individual contracts","Labour market flexibility and productivity","Microeconomic reform and the non-accelerating inflation rate of unemployment (NAIRU)","Short-run adjustment costs of structural change"],U4t={id:"economics/2023/q24",version:1,variants:1,syllabusTopics:[...D4t],learningTopics:W4t,generate(t){return{question:[],parts:[{question:"A paper mill in a regional town closes, and 600 machine operators lose their jobs. At the same time, local hospitals and solar installers report that they cannot find enough qualified staff. Outline how a government-funded retraining program could benefit the economy in this situation.",sampleAnswer:["The former mill workers are **structurally unemployed**: jobs exist in the town, but their skills do not match the vacancies in health care and solar installation.","","A retraining program, such as subsidised courses in aged-care support or electrical installation, closes this skills gap. Workers move back into employment sooner, which lifts output and incomes, reduces spending on unemployment benefits and eases the labour shortages that were holding back growing industries. It also stops long-term unemployment from eroding workers’ skills further."].join(`
`),marks:2},{question:"A software company is hiring a data engineer. Rather than applying an award rate, the company and the engineer negotiate an individual contract covering pay, working hours and performance bonuses. Explain how this method of setting employment conditions could benefit both the employer and the employee.",sampleAnswer:["**Benefits to the employer.** An individual contract lets the company tailor pay and conditions to this role and this worker. It can link part of the salary to performance through bonuses, which encourages higher productivity, and it can pay above the market to attract and keep a scarce specialist without having to raise pay for every staff member. Flexible hours can also be arranged around project deadlines.","","**Benefits to the employee.** A worker with skills that are in high demand has strong bargaining power, so they can negotiate a salary above the award or agreement rate that reflects their productivity. They can also trade off conditions to suit their circumstances, for example accepting a performance bonus in return for the option to work from home.","","Both sides gain because the contract matches pay more closely to the value of the work. The benefit to employees depends on bargaining power: workers with less sought-after skills may end up with worse conditions than a collective agreement would provide."].join(`
`),marks:3},{question:"Assess how effective microeconomic reform has been as a way of reducing unemployment in Australia.",sampleAnswer:["Microeconomic reform aims to raise efficiency and productivity at the level of industries and markets. Examples include tariff cuts, competition policy, privatisation, deregulation of industries such as aviation and telecommunications, and labour market reform such as the shift from centralised wage fixing to enterprise bargaining.","","**How reform reduces unemployment.** Reform lowers costs and increases competitiveness, allowing firms to expand output and employment. A more flexible labour market lets wages respond to conditions in each firm and industry, so workers are re-employed more quickly after a shock. Together these changes reduce structural unemployment and lower the NAIRU, so the economy can sustain a lower unemployment rate without accelerating inflation.","","**Evidence of effectiveness.** Australia’s unemployment rate fell from above 10 per cent in the early 1990s recession to around 5 per cent before the pandemic, and to about 3½ per cent in 2022–23, without a lasting surge in wage inflation. Many economists attribute part of the lower NAIRU to the reforms of the 1980s and 1990s.","","**Limitations.** Reform works slowly, and its short-run effect can be to increase unemployment. When tariffs were cut, jobs were lost in the textiles, clothing, footwear and car-manufacturing industries, often in regions with few alternative jobs. Displaced workers with narrow skills may stay out of work for long periods unless reform is supported by training and job-search programs. It is also hard to isolate reform’s contribution, because strong terms of trade, population growth and expansionary macroeconomic policy also lowered unemployment over this period.","","**Judgement.** Microeconomic reform has been moderately effective. It has not been the main tool for managing cyclical unemployment, which depends on fiscal and monetary policy, but it has helped lower the unemployment rate the economy can sustain over the long run. Its effectiveness is greatest when it is combined with labour market programs that help workers shift from declining to growing industries."].join(`
`),marks:5}]}}},Y4t=["EC-H2","EC-H3"],G4t=["Value, composition and direction of Australia’s trade","Terms of trade and commodity price cycles","Current account balance and external stability","Resources boom and structural change (two-speed economy)","Services exports: international education and tourism","Trade concentration risk and Asia–Pacific trading partners"],V4t={id:"economics/2023/q25",version:1,variants:1,syllabusTopics:[...Y4t],learningTopics:G4t,generate(t){return{question:["With reference to the information below, analyse the impact that shifts in the value, composition and direction of Australia’s trade have had on the performance of the Australian economy.","","**Recent trends in Australia’s trade (summary prepared for this question)**","","- Resource and energy commodities such as iron ore, coal and liquefied natural gas account for more than half of the value of Australia’s goods exports.","- Australia’s terms of trade reached a record high in 2022, following large rises in the world prices of coal and gas.","- In 2019 the current account moved into surplus for the first time since the mid-1970s, and it remained in surplus for several years.","- Exports of international education and tourism services fell sharply when international borders closed in 2020–21, then recovered after borders reopened.","- China has been Australia’s largest export destination for more than a decade, and East and South Asian economies together buy most of Australia’s exports."].join(`
`),sampleAnswer:["**Introduction and judgement.** Over the past two decades Australia’s trade has become more valuable, more concentrated in resources and more focused on Asia. These shifts have generally strengthened economic performance by raising national income, supporting growth and improving external stability, but they have also made the economy more exposed to commodity price cycles and to conditions in a small number of trading partners.","","**Value of trade.** The value of exports depends on both volumes and prices. The rise in world coal and gas prices pushed the terms of trade to a record high in 2022, so each unit of exports paid for more imports. Higher export prices raised company profits, wages in the resources sector and national income, and increased company tax and royalty revenue, which helped improve the federal budget position. Higher export earnings were the main reason the current account moved into surplus from 2019, ending more than four decades of deficits. A surplus means Australia was a net lender to the rest of the world, which reduced reliance on foreign borrowing and improved external stability. The effect on growth is less direct: a price-driven rise in export value lifts nominal GDP and incomes more than real output, and a high terms of trade also tends to push up the Australian dollar.","","**Composition of trade.** Australia’s export mix has shifted towards minerals and energy, while manufactured exports have declined as a share of the total. Rural goods remain important, and services exports, especially international education and tourism, grew strongly until 2020. The concentration on resources has benefited economic performance in boom periods by attracting large mining investment, creating high-paid jobs in Western Australia and Queensland, and lifting productivity in the sector. However, it has also produced a two-speed economy: a strong dollar during resource booms reduced the competitiveness of manufacturing, tourism and education, and contributed to job losses in trade-exposed industries. The pandemic showed the vulnerability of services exports: when borders closed, education and tourism receipts collapsed, hurting universities, hospitality and regional economies, even while resource exports stayed strong. A narrow export base therefore makes the economy more exposed to swings in commodity prices, which can quickly reverse the gains in income and the current account.","","**Direction of trade.** Australia’s trade has shifted from Europe and the United States towards Asia. China has been the largest single market for more than a decade, driven by its demand for iron ore to build infrastructure and housing, and Japan, South Korea and India are also major buyers. Close links with fast-growing Asian economies have given Australia a strong source of demand, helping it avoid recession during the global financial crisis and supporting growth through the 2010s. The concentration of exports in one market is also a risk. When China imposed restrictions on Australian barley, wine, coal and other goods from 2020, exporters in these industries lost sales and had to find new markets, although strong iron ore prices limited the overall damage. A slowdown in China’s construction sector could reduce both the volume and price of Australia’s largest export.","","**Links between the changes.** The three trends reinforce each other. The shift in direction towards industrialising Asia is the main reason the composition has moved towards resources, and Asian demand drives the commodity prices that determine the value of trade. This means the gains to growth, income and the current account have all depended on the same underlying source of demand.","","**Conclusion.** Changes in the value, composition and direction of trade have improved Australia’s economic performance, most clearly through higher national income and a stronger external position after 2019. The benefits have been uneven across industries and regions, and they rest on commodity prices and Asian demand that Australia cannot control. Diversifying exports, for example through services, critical minerals and new markets in South and South-East Asia, would reduce this vulnerability.","","Full marks need an analysis of all three aspects (value, composition and direction), each linked to specific measures of performance such as growth, national income, the current account and employment, with the stimulus used as evidence and a clear judgement."].join(`
`),marks:20}}},X4t=["EC-H4","EC-H3"],K4t=["Environmental sustainability and ecologically sustainable development","Negative externalities and market failure in energy production","Structural change and regional structural unemployment","Resource exports, the terms of trade and the current account","Market-based and regulatory environmental policies","Trade-offs between environmental sustainability and economic growth"],Q4t={id:"economics/2023/q26",version:1,variants:1,syllabusTopics:[...X4t],learningTopics:K4t,generate(t){return{question:["Using the information provided, analyse the likely economic effects for Australia of pursuing environmental sustainability.","","Many of Australia’s trading partners have committed to reaching net-zero emissions by around 2050. Within Australia, governments are encouraging investment in wind and solar generation, battery storage and transmission lines, while older coal-fired power stations are scheduled to close.","","The table shows **hypothetical** projections, constructed for this question, of Australia’s coal exports under two scenarios for world demand.","","| Year | Coal exports if trading partners keep current policies (million tonnes) | Coal exports if trading partners reach net zero by 2050 (million tonnes) |","| --- | --- | --- |","| 2025 | 340 | 340 |","| 2030 | 330 | 250 |","| 2040 | 310 | 120 |","| 2050 | 290 | 30 |"].join(`
`),sampleAnswer:["**Introduction and judgement.** Environmental sustainability means meeting present needs without reducing the ability of future generations to meet theirs, which requires the economy to reduce pollution and the depletion of natural resources. For Australia, a large exporter of fossil fuels with an emissions-intensive electricity system, pursuing this goal involves significant short- to medium-term costs in some industries and regions, but it also creates new sources of growth and avoids larger long-run costs from climate change.","","**Market failure as the starting point.** Burning coal and gas creates negative externalities: the costs of emissions and climate damage are not included in the price paid by producers and consumers. Without intervention, fossil-fuel energy is overproduced. Policies such as emissions limits on large polluters (for example, the Safeguard Mechanism), renewable energy targets, subsidies for clean technology and regulation of power station closures correct this by raising the relative cost of polluting activities. This shifts resources towards low-emission production.","","**Effects on trade and the external sector.** The table shows that if trading partners reach net zero, coal exports fall from 340 million tonnes in 2025 to 30 million tonnes by 2050, a fall of about 91 per cent, compared with about 15 per cent under current policies. By 2040 the gap between the scenarios is already 190 million tonnes. Coal is one of Australia’s largest export earners, so this would reduce export income, weaken the terms of trade and the current account balance, and put downward pressure on the Australian dollar. Company tax and state royalty revenue would also fall. On the other hand, a lower dollar would make other exports more competitive, and global decarbonisation raises demand for critical minerals such as lithium, copper and rare earths, as well as for green hydrogen and green metals, where Australia has natural advantages.","","**Effects on growth, investment and structural change.** Pursuing sustainability requires large investment in renewable generation, storage and transmission, which adds to aggregate demand and creates jobs in construction, engineering and electrical trades. In the long run, cheap renewable energy could lower production costs and support new energy-intensive industries. In the transition, however, resources must move out of coal mining and coal-fired power. Capital in these industries may become stranded, and output may grow more slowly while new industries are established.","","**Effects on employment and regions.** The costs of the transition are concentrated. Coal mining regions such as the Hunter Valley, Central Queensland and Victoria’s Latrobe Valley depend heavily on coal mining or coal-fired power, so a rapid fall in coal exports like the net-zero scenario, together with power station closures, would cause structural unemployment there. Workers’ skills may not match the new jobs, which may be in other locations. Governments would need retraining programs and regional assistance to reduce these costs.","","**Effects on prices and the budget.** The transition may add to costs in the short term. Closing coal plants before enough replacement capacity is built can raise wholesale electricity prices, adding to cost-push inflation and to costs for energy-intensive firms. Governments may also face higher spending on transmission infrastructure, industry assistance and household energy rebates at the same time as coal revenue falls.","","**Long-run benefits.** Sustainability reduces the future economic costs of climate change, such as damage to agriculture, infrastructure and tourism from droughts, floods and bushfires, and damage to natural assets such as the Great Barrier Reef. Cleaner air improves health and productivity. These benefits are not always captured by GDP, but they raise quality of life and protect the resource base for future growth.","","**Conclusion.** Pursuing environmental sustainability is likely to reduce output, export income and employment in fossil-fuel industries, particularly under the net-zero scenario in the table, and it may add to inflation and budget pressures during the transition. Because Australia’s trading partners are decarbonising anyway, much of the fall in coal demand will occur regardless of Australian policy. On balance, an orderly transition that builds new export industries and supports affected regions is likely to deliver long-run economic gains that outweigh the short-term costs.","","Full marks need analysis of both costs and benefits across several parts of the economy (trade, growth, employment, prices, the budget and living standards), with the table used as evidence and a supported judgement."].join(`
`),marks:20}}},J4t=["EC-H1"],Z4t=["Distinction between economic growth and economic development","Foreign direct investment and multinational corporations in developing economies","Export-oriented industrialisation and trade agreements","Human Development Index and poverty measures","Regional and rural–urban inequality in a developing economy","Environmental costs of rapid industrialisation","Exposure of open economies to external shocks"],eAt={id:"economics/2023/q27",version:1,variants:1,syllabusTopics:[...J4t],learningTopics:Z4t,generate(t){return{question:"With reference to a country other than Australia, evaluate how successfully globalisation has helped that economy achieve both economic growth and economic development.",sampleAnswer:["This model answer uses Vietnam. Any economy other than Australia is acceptable, provided the answer gives specific evidence about it.","","**Introduction and judgement.** Globalisation is the increasing integration of national economies through trade, foreign investment, financial flows, technology and the movement of people. Economic growth is an increase in real output, while economic development is a broader improvement in living standards, including health, education, income distribution and the environment. Since its Đổi Mới reforms of 1986 opened the economy to world markets, Vietnam has been one of the clearest examples of globalisation producing rapid growth. It has also delivered major development gains, although these have been uneven and have come with environmental and external risks.","","**Globalisation and economic growth.** Vietnam’s integration with the global economy has been the main driver of its growth. It joined the World Trade Organization in 2007 and has signed agreements such as the Comprehensive and Progressive Agreement for Trans-Pacific Partnership (CPTPP) and a free trade agreement with the European Union. Lower trade barriers and low labour costs attracted large inflows of foreign direct investment from multinationals such as Samsung, Intel and major garment and footwear producers. Exports of electronics, clothing and footwear expanded rapidly, and trade is now larger than GDP. Real GDP growth has averaged roughly 6 to 7 per cent a year for most of the past three decades. FDI brought capital, technology and management skills, lifting productivity as workers moved from low-productivity farming into manufacturing.","","**Globalisation and economic development.** Growth has translated into significant development gains. Rising employment in export industries raised household incomes, and extreme poverty fell from more than half of the population in the early 1990s to under 2 per cent by the early 2020s. Higher incomes and government revenue funded improvements in schooling, health care, electricity and clean water, and Vietnam’s Human Development Index and life expectancy have risen steadily. Vietnam moved from low-income to lower-middle-income status in around 2010 and was reclassified by the World Bank as upper-middle-income in 2026. Its school students perform well in international tests.","","**Limits and costs.**","","- **Uneven distribution of gains.** Benefits have been concentrated in the export hubs around Hanoi and Ho Chi Minh City. Ethnic minority communities in the northern mountains and Central Highlands have much higher poverty rates, and the gap between urban and rural incomes remains large.","- **Environmental damage.** Rapid industrialisation, coal-fired power and urbanisation have caused serious air pollution in major cities, water pollution in industrial zones and pressure on the Mekong Delta. These costs reduce quality of life and are not reflected in GDP.","- **Low value-added and dependence on foreign firms.** Much of Vietnam’s export production is assembly of imported components by foreign multinationals. Domestic firms capture a relatively small share of the value created, and a large share of exports comes from a handful of companies, so profits flow overseas and domestic technological capability develops slowly.","- **Exposure to external shocks.** As a highly open economy, Vietnam is vulnerable to downturns in its major markets, disruptions to global supply chains and changes in trade policy, such as tariffs imposed by the United States, its largest export market.","","**Evaluation.** Weighing these points, globalisation has been highly successful in generating economic growth and substantially successful in promoting development, particularly through poverty reduction and better access to education and health care. Its success in development is qualified by regional inequality and environmental damage, which show that growth alone does not guarantee sustainable, broad-based development. Vietnam’s future development depends on domestic policies that spread the gains, such as investment in rural infrastructure, skills and environmental regulation, and on moving into higher value-added production.","","Full marks need a clear distinction between growth and development, specific evidence about the chosen economy, and a judgement that weighs the benefits against the costs."].join(`
`),marks:20}}},tAt=["EC-H4","EC-H3"],nAt=["Monetary policy transmission and the cash rate","Inflation targeting and the Reserve Bank’s 2–3 per cent target","Fiscal stance, automatic stabilisers and discretionary stimulus","Policy responses to the COVID-19 recession and post-pandemic inflation","Supply-side and demand-pull causes of inflation","Time lags and conflicts between growth and price stability"],iAt={id:"economics/2023/q28",version:1,variants:1,syllabusTopics:[...tAt],learningTopics:nAt,generate(t){return{question:"Evaluate how successfully fiscal and monetary policy have promoted sustainable economic growth and low inflation in Australia in recent years.",sampleAnswer:["**Introduction and judgement.** Macroeconomic policy manages aggregate demand to smooth the business cycle. Monetary policy, set by the Reserve Bank of Australia (RBA), targets consumer price inflation of 2–3 per cent on average, while fiscal policy, set by the Australian Government through the budget, supports growth and employment. Since 2020, these policies were highly successful in preventing a prolonged recession, but they were less successful in keeping inflation low, and bringing inflation back to target has come at the cost of slower growth.","","**Response to the pandemic recession.** In 2020, lockdowns caused the largest quarterly fall in real GDP since quarterly records began in 1959, 7 per cent in the June quarter. Both policies were strongly expansionary. The RBA cut the cash rate to a record low of 0.1 per cent, bought government bonds to lower longer-term interest rates and provided cheap funding to banks. Fiscal policy delivered the largest stimulus in Australia’s history, including the JobKeeper wage subsidy, higher JobSeeker payments and investment incentives for business, pushing the budget into a deficit of more than 6 per cent of GDP. The policy mix was effective: the recession was short, the economy regained its pre-pandemic output within about a year, and unemployment fell to around 3½ per cent in 2022, the lowest rate in almost 50 years.","","**Inflation and the monetary policy response.** Policy was less successful in maintaining price stability. Inflation rose sharply from 2021, reaching 7.8 per cent over the year to the December quarter of 2022, well above the target band. Much of this came from supply-side shocks that domestic policy cannot directly control, such as disrupted global supply chains, higher energy prices after Russia’s invasion of Ukraine, and floods affecting food prices. However, demand-pull pressure also contributed. Interest rates were kept at emergency levels until May 2022 and fiscal support continued while the labour market was already tight. From May 2022 the RBA raised the cash rate thirteen times to 4.35 per cent by November 2023. Higher mortgage repayments reduced household spending and inflation fell, with headline inflation back within the target band by late 2024. This shows that monetary policy can control inflation, but only with long time lags and after a period of inflation well above target.","","**Fiscal policy after the recovery.** Stronger revenue from employment growth and high commodity prices, together with restraint on new spending, allowed the government to record budget surpluses in 2022–23 and 2023–24, the first since 2007–08. This contractionary stance supported monetary policy in reducing inflation. Some targeted measures, such as energy bill rebates, lowered measured inflation directly. Critics argue that other measures, such as tax cuts and higher government consumption, added to demand while the RBA was trying to slow it, so the two arms of policy were not always pulling in the same direction.","","**The cost to growth.** Tight monetary policy slowed the economy. Household consumption was weak, real incomes fell as prices rose faster than wages, and real GDP per person declined over much of 2023 and 2024 even though total output kept growing because of strong population growth. Unemployment rose only modestly, which suggests the RBA managed to reduce inflation without a recession, but living standards were squeezed.","","**Constraints on effectiveness.** Macroeconomic policy mainly works on demand, so it has limited ability to address supply shocks. Time lags of a year or more make it difficult to judge the right setting. Also, weak productivity growth limits the rate at which the economy can grow without generating inflation, which is a problem for microeconomic rather than macroeconomic policy.","","**Conclusion.** Fiscal and monetary policy were very effective in protecting growth and employment during the pandemic. They were slower to respond to the rise in inflation, partly because it began as a supply shock and partly because stimulus was withdrawn late. Once tightened, policy returned inflation towards the target without a recession, but at the cost of several years of falling real incomes and weak per capita growth. Overall, policy has been effective in managing the business cycle, but sustaining non-inflationary growth now depends more on productivity-enhancing reform.","","Full marks need an evaluation of both fiscal and monetary policy against both objectives, using recent Australian data, and a judgement that recognises the conflict between growth and price stability."].join(`
`),marks:20}}},aAt=[_5t,O5t,F5t,H5t,z5t,U5t,V5t,Q5t,e4t,i4t,s4t,c4t,u4t,f4t,y4t,v4t,T4t,S4t,_4t,O4t],oAt=[F4t,H4t,z4t,U4t,V4t,Q4t,eAt,iAt],Wi=[{variants:he({question:["A low-income country wants long-term finance to build rural health clinics, clean water systems and primary schools. Its goal is to reduce poverty and raise living standards.","","Which international organisation exists mainly to fund and advise on projects like these?"].join(`
`),choices:["The World Bank","The International Monetary Fund (IMF)","The World Trade Organization (WTO)","The Organisation for Economic Co-operation and Development (OECD)"],correctChoiceIndex:0,explanation:["Each of these institutions has a different core role, so match the role to the goal in the stem: long-term development and poverty reduction.","","- The **World Bank** provides long-term loans, grants and technical advice to developing countries for projects in health, education, water and infrastructure. Its stated aim is to reduce poverty and improve living standards, so it fits the stem.","- The **IMF** looks after the stability of the international financial system. It lends mainly to countries facing a balance of payments or currency crisis, usually on condition of economic reforms, rather than funding individual clinics or schools.","- The **WTO** sets and enforces the rules of international trade and settles trade disputes. It does not lend money.","- The **OECD** is a forum of mostly high-income economies that researches and shares policy advice. It does not finance development projects."].join(`
`)})},{variants:he({question:["In a centralised system of wage determination, an independent industrial tribunal sets minimum pay rates and conditions that apply across whole industries or the whole economy.","","Which of the following is an advantage of this system?"].join(`
`),choices:["It lets wages in each firm respond quickly to that firm’s productivity and profits","It speeds up the movement of labour from declining industries to growing ones","It guarantees minimum pay and conditions for workers with little bargaining power","It widens the gap between high-income and low-income earners"],correctChoiceIndex:2,explanation:["Centralised wage setting, such as awards and the national minimum wage set by the Fair Work Commission in Australia, applies the same minimum standards to many workers at once.","","- Guaranteeing minimum pay and conditions is its main benefit. Young, low-skilled and casual workers have little power to bargain on their own, so a centrally set floor acts as a **safety net** and supports equity.","- Wages that respond to each firm’s productivity and profits are a feature of **decentralised** (enterprise or individual) bargaining. Centralised systems are usually criticised for being inflexible.","- Labour moves between industries when relative wages change. Uniform, economy-wide wage rises weaken these signals, so faster structural change is an argument for decentralisation, not a benefit of centralisation.","- Wider income inequality is a cost, not a benefit, and it is linked to decentralised systems, where workers with strong bargaining power gain more than those with weak bargaining power."].join(`
`)})},{variants:he({question:"Which pair of economic objectives is most likely to involve a trade-off in the short run, so that progress towards one makes the other harder to achieve?",choices:["Low unemployment and low inflation","Faster economic growth and lower unemployment","Low inflation and stronger international competitiveness","A more equal distribution of income and a smaller current account deficit"],correctChoiceIndex:0,explanation:["The short-run trade-off between inflation and unemployment, shown by the Phillips curve, is the classic conflict between economic objectives.","","When aggregate demand grows strongly, firms hire more workers and unemployment falls. As the labour market tightens and spare capacity is used up, wages and prices rise faster, so inflation increases. Policies that reduce inflation, such as higher interest rates, slow demand and push unemployment up. So full employment and price stability pull against each other in the short run.","","- Faster growth and lower unemployment are **complementary**: producing more output needs more labour, so growth usually reduces unemployment.","- Low inflation helps competitiveness: when domestic prices rise more slowly than overseas prices, local goods become relatively cheaper, so these objectives support each other.","- Income equality and the current account deficit are not directly linked in the short run, so there is no clear trade-off between them."].join(`
`)})},{variants:he({question:["A country removes all of its tariffs and import quotas as part of a move to free trade.","","Which of the following is most likely to be a **cost** of this decision for the domestic economy?"].join(`
`),choices:["Local firms are forced to cut costs and become more efficient","Consumers pay higher prices for imported goods","Overseas firms can more easily sell goods below their normal price, damaging local producers","Exporting firms expand output and lower their average costs"],correctChoiceIndex:2,explanation:["Free trade brings both gains and costs, so sort each option into one group or the other.","","- **Dumping** is when a foreign firm sells exports below the price it charges at home, or below its cost of production, often to get rid of surplus output. Without tariffs or quotas, local producers are exposed to this unfair competition, which can cut their output and jobs even if they are efficient. This is a cost of free trade. Countries can still respond with anti-dumping duties under WTO rules.","- Pressure on local firms to become more efficient is a **benefit** of the greater competition that free trade brings.","- Removing tariffs makes imports **cheaper**, not dearer, so higher import prices are the opposite of what happens.","- Access to larger markets lets exporters gain **economies of scale**, which is another benefit."].join(`
`)})},{variants:he({question:["A superannuation fund based in Canada buys 3% of the ordinary shares of an Australian bank listed on the ASX.","","How is this transaction recorded in Australia’s balance of payments?"].join(`
`),choices:["A credit to direct investment in the financial account","A debit to portfolio investment in the financial account","A debit to direct investment in the financial account","A credit to portfolio investment in the financial account"],correctChoiceIndex:3,explanation:["Two decisions are needed: which category of investment it is, and whether it is a credit or a debit.","","**Category.** Foreign investment counts as direct investment when the investor gains a lasting interest in, and some control over, the business. The balance of payments measures this as owning **10% or more** of the ordinary shares. A 3% holding is below this threshold, so it is **portfolio investment**.","","**Direction.** The Canadian fund must buy Australian dollars to pay for the shares, so money flows **into** Australia. An inflow of foreign capital adds to Australia’s foreign liabilities and is recorded as a **credit**.","","The direct investment options use the wrong category, because 3% is too small a share to give control. The debit options treat the transaction as an outflow, which would apply if an Australian investor bought shares in an overseas company."].join(`
`)})},{variants:he({question:["The table shows labour market data for an imaginary economy.","","| Measure | Number of people |","| --- | ---: |","| Total population | 25,000,000 |","| Working-age population (15 years and over) | 20,000,000 |","| Employed | 12,600,000 |","| Unemployed | 900,000 |","","What is the labour force participation rate?"].join(`
`),choices:["54.0%","63.0%","50.4%","67.5%"],correctChoiceIndex:3,explanation:["The participation rate is the share of the working-age population that is in the labour force, meaning either employed or unemployed and actively looking for work.","","$$\\text{Labour force} = 12{,}600{,}000 + 900{,}000 = 13{,}500{,}000$$","","$$\\text{Participation rate} = \\frac{\\text{labour force}}{\\text{working-age population}} \\times 100 = \\frac{13{,}500{,}000}{20{,}000{,}000} \\times 100 = 67.5\\%$$","","The other options use the wrong numerator or denominator:","- 54.0% divides the labour force by the **total** population. Children below working age cannot be in the labour force, so they must be left out.","- 63.0% divides only the employed by the working-age population. This ignores the unemployed, who are part of the labour force; it is the employment-to-population ratio instead.","- 50.4% divides the employed by the total population, making both errors."].join(`
`)})},{variants:he({question:["An imaginary economy recorded the following national income and saving.","","| Year | National income (AUD million) | Saving (AUD million) |","| --- | ---: | ---: |","| 1 | 500 | 200 |","| 2 | 750 | 250 |","","Calculate the marginal propensity to consume (MPC)."].join(`
`),choices:["0.20","0.67","0.80","0.33"],correctChoiceIndex:2,explanation:["The MPC is the fraction of each **extra** dollar of income that is spent on consumption, so it uses the changes in income and saving, not the levels in one year.","","$$\\text{MPS} = \\frac{\\Delta S}{\\Delta Y} = \\frac{250 - 200}{750 - 500} = \\frac{50}{250} = 0.20$$","","Every extra dollar of income is either consumed or saved, so $\\text{MPC} + \\text{MPS} = 1$:","","$$\\text{MPC} = 1 - 0.20 = 0.80$$","","- 0.20 is the MPS, the share of extra income that is saved rather than consumed.","- 0.67 is the **average** propensity to consume in Year 2, $(750 - 250) \\div 750$. It divides total consumption by total income instead of using the changes.","- 0.33 is the average propensity to save in Year 2, $250 \\div 750$, which makes both errors."].join(`
`)})},{variants:he({question:"Once policymakers agree that the economy needs a change of policy, which of the following can usually be put into effect the most quickly?",choices:["Microeconomic reform","Monetary policy","Fiscal policy","Environmental regulation"],correctChoiceIndex:1,explanation:["The implementation lag is the time between deciding that a policy change is needed and actually putting it in place. It is different from the impact lag, which is how long the change takes to affect the economy.","","- **Monetary policy** has the shortest implementation lag. The Reserve Bank’s board meets regularly through the year and can change the cash rate target at any meeting, with the new rate applying almost immediately. It does not need to pass through Parliament.","- **Fiscal policy** is mostly changed in the annual Budget, and new taxes or spending programs often need legislation to pass Parliament, so it is slower to implement.","- **Microeconomic reform**, such as changes to competition policy, industrial relations or training, involves consultation, legislation and negotiation with states or industries, and can take years.","- **Environmental regulation** also needs laws, consultation and monitoring systems before it can take effect.","","Monetary policy does have a relatively **long impact lag**: a cash rate change may take a year or more to have its full effect on spending and inflation."].join(`
`)})},{variants:he({question:["The government launches a free national website that quickly matches job seekers with suitable vacancies, based on their skills and location.","","Which of the following is this change most likely to reduce?"].join(`
`),choices:["Cyclical unemployment","Seasonal unemployment","The labour force participation rate","Frictional unemployment"],correctChoiceIndex:3,explanation:["Frictional unemployment is the short-term unemployment of people who are between jobs or entering the workforce and are still searching for a suitable position. It exists partly because it takes time for job seekers and employers to find each other.","","A faster, better-informed matching service cuts the time that each person spends searching, so it directly reduces **frictional** unemployment.","","- **Cyclical** unemployment is caused by too little aggregate demand during a downturn. If there are not enough vacancies, better matching cannot create the missing jobs; stronger demand is needed.","- **Seasonal** unemployment arises because some work, such as fruit picking or ski resort jobs, exists only at certain times of the year. Faster matching does not change when this work is available.","- The **participation rate** would, if anything, rise slightly, because easier job searching may encourage discouraged workers to start looking again. It would not be reduced."].join(`
`)})},{variants:he({question:["The Reserve Bank of Australia is concerned that the Australian dollar has fallen too far.","","Which combination of actions would put the most upward pressure on the Australian dollar?"].join(`
`),choices:["Lower the cash rate target and use foreign currency reserves to buy Australian dollars","Raise the cash rate target and use foreign currency reserves to buy Australian dollars","Raise the cash rate target and sell Australian dollars in exchange for foreign currency","Lower the cash rate target and sell Australian dollars in exchange for foreign currency"],correctChoiceIndex:1,explanation:["The value of the dollar depends on the demand for and supply of Australian dollars in the foreign exchange market, so both actions must raise demand for the currency.","","- **Raising the cash rate** lifts Australian interest rates relative to overseas rates. Higher returns attract foreign savings into Australian financial assets, and foreign investors must buy Australian dollars to invest, so demand for the dollar rises.","- **Buying Australian dollars** in the FOREX market with foreign currency reserves adds directly to demand for the dollar. The RBA has used this kind of intervention to steady the currency during sharp falls.","","Together these cause an appreciation. Lowering the cash rate reduces the return on Australian assets and encourages capital to flow out, and selling Australian dollars increases their supply. Both push the dollar down, so every other option contains at least one action that works against the goal."].join(`
`)})},{variants:he({question:["The diagram shows aggregate supply in an economy shifting from AS₁ to AS₂, while aggregate demand (AD) is unchanged.",Gn({xMax:110,yMax:100,xTicks:[],yTicks:[],xLabel:"Real GDP",yLabel:"Price level",curves:[{from:[10,90],to:[90,10],label:"AD"},{from:[15,5],to:[90,80],label:'AS<tspan baseline-shift="sub" font-size="11">1</tspan>'},{from:[35,5],to:[100,70],label:'AS<tspan baseline-shift="sub" font-size="11">2</tspan>'}]}),"Which change would be the most likely cause of this shift?"],choices:["Lower fees for university and TAFE courses","A rise in the goods and services tax rate to 15%","Fewer migrants arriving in the economy","Higher government spending on the aged pension"],correctChoiceIndex:0,explanation:["This question tests the factors that shift aggregate supply, as opposed to those that shift aggregate demand.","","AS₂ lies to the right of AS₁, so the productive capacity of the economy has **increased**: more real output can be supplied at every price level.","","- Lower fees for tertiary study encourage more people to gain skills and qualifications. A more skilled workforce is more productive, so AS shifts right. This matches.","- A higher GST rate raises prices and costs across the economy, so it tends to reduce aggregate demand and, if anything, shift AS left.","- Fewer migrants means a smaller labour force, which shifts AS left.","- Aged pension spending is government expenditure on transfers. It affects aggregate demand, not productive capacity.","","Clue: ask whether each change affects the quantity or quality of the factors of production (supply side) or only spending (demand side)."].join(`
`)})},{variants:he({question:["The table shows balance of payments items for an economy with a floating exchange rate. Net errors and omissions are zero.","","| Item | AUD billion |","| --- | ---: |","| Exports of goods | ? |","| Imports of goods | −210 |","| Net services | −15 |","| Net primary income | −48 |","| Net secondary income | −3 |","| Capital account balance | −1 |","| Financial account balance | 62 |","","What is the value of exports of goods?"].join(`
`),choices:["AUD 276 billion","AUD 339 billion","AUD 164 billion","AUD 215 billion"],correctChoiceIndex:3,explanation:["With a floating exchange rate the balance of payments must balance: a current account deficit is matched by a net inflow on the capital and financial account.","","$$\\text{CA} + \\text{KA} + \\text{FA} = 0$$","","The capital and financial account together total $-1 + 62 = 61$, so the current account must be $-61$ billion. Write the current account in terms of the unknown exports, $X$:","","$$X - 210 - 15 - 48 - 3 = -61$$","","$$X - 276 = -61 \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad X = 215$$","","Exports of goods are AUD 215 billion. Check: the balance on goods is $215 - 210 = 5$, and adding the other current account items gives $5 - 15 - 48 - 3 = -61$, which is exactly offset by the net inflow of 61 on the capital and financial account.","","- AUD 276 billion assumes the current account must balance on its own, ignoring the capital and financial account.","- AUD 339 billion treats the financial account inflow as if it added to the deficit, instead of offsetting it.","- AUD 164 billion leaves out net primary and secondary income, which are part of the current account."].join(`
`)})},{variants:he({question:["A government plans to tighten local content rules for television streaming services, so that a larger share of the programs they show must be made in the country.","","Which option shows the most likely effect on imports and on short-term employment opportunities in this economy?"].join(`
`),choices:["Imports rise; short-term employment opportunities increase","Imports rise; short-term employment opportunities decrease","Imports fall; short-term employment opportunities increase","Imports fall; short-term employment opportunities decrease"],correctChoiceIndex:2,explanation:["This question tests local content rules as a form of protection, and their short-run effects.","","- **Imports:** streaming services must show more locally made programs, so they buy fewer overseas programs. Imports of these services **fall**.","- **Short-term employment:** more local programs must be produced, so demand rises for local actors, writers, film crews and production firms. Short-term employment opportunities **increase**.","","Like other forms of protection, these gains in the protected industry can come with longer-run costs, such as higher prices or less choice for consumers and less pressure on local producers to become efficient. The question only asks about the short term."].join(`
`)})},{variants:he({question:["Between Year 1 and Year 2, the Gini coefficient for household disposable income in an economy fell from 0.41 to 0.33.","","Which combination of government policy changes is most likely to have contributed to this change?"].join(`
`),choices:["Rent assistance for low-income tenants increased; tax-free threshold raised","Rent assistance for low-income tenants increased; tax-free threshold lowered","Rent assistance for low-income tenants reduced; tax-free threshold raised","Rent assistance for low-income tenants reduced; tax-free threshold lowered"],correctChoiceIndex:0,explanation:["The Gini coefficient ranges from 0 (perfect equality) to 1 (perfect inequality). A fall from 0.41 to 0.33 means disposable income became **more equally** distributed, so look for policies that raise the disposable income of low-income households relative to high-income households.","","- **More rent assistance** is a transfer payment targeted at low-income tenants. It adds directly to their disposable income, which reduces inequality.","- **A higher tax-free threshold** means people pay no income tax on a larger slice of their income. The tax saving is a much bigger share of income for a low-income earner than for a high-income earner, and some low-income earners stop paying tax altogether, so the tax system becomes more progressive.","","Cutting rent assistance or lowering the tax-free threshold would reduce the disposable income of lower-income households and push the Gini coefficient **up**. The mixed options have one change working against the other, so they are much less likely to explain such a large fall."].join(`
`)})},{variants:he({question:"Which option correctly pairs a cyclical factor and a structural factor that influence the size of Australia’s net primary income deficit?",choices:["Cyclical: swings in world commodity prices; structural: a narrow export base","Cyclical: changes in world interest rates; structural: low national saving relative to investment","Cyclical: a long-term reliance on foreign capital; structural: movements in the cash rate","Cyclical: changes in the number of overseas tourists; structural: the international competitiveness of manufacturing"],correctChoiceIndex:1,explanation:["Net primary income records income paid to and received from overseas, mainly interest on foreign debt and dividends on foreign equity. Australia runs a large deficit on this account because it has borrowed heavily and attracted a lot of foreign investment over many decades.","","- **Cyclical factor, world interest rates:** interest rates rise and fall with the economic cycle. When they rise, the cost of servicing Australia’s foreign debt rises and the net primary income deficit widens; when they fall, it narrows.","- **Structural factor, low national saving:** Australia has long invested more than it saves. The gap is filled by foreign borrowing and investment, which builds up foreign liabilities and a lasting stream of interest and dividend payments overseas.","","Commodity prices and a narrow export base mainly affect the **balance on goods and services**, not income flows. Tourist numbers and manufacturing competitiveness also affect trade in goods and services rather than net primary income. The option that calls reliance on foreign capital cyclical and the cash rate structural has the labels reversed: reliance on foreign capital is long-term, while the cash rate moves with the cycle."].join(`
`)})},{variants:he({question:["Over the past year, headline CPI inflation in an economy jumped to 5.8%, while the trimmed mean measure of underlying inflation stayed close to 3%.","","Which event is most likely to explain this pattern?"].join(`
`),choices:["A sharp fall in world oil prices lowers petrol prices","Wages grow steadily faster across the service industries","Rents rise gradually in the major cities","Conflict in a major oil-exporting region causes a sudden spike in world oil prices"],correctChoiceIndex:3,explanation:["Headline inflation is the change in the price of the whole CPI basket. Underlying measures such as the trimmed mean remove the items with the most extreme price changes in each period, so they show the persistent trend. When headline inflation runs well above underlying inflation, a few prices have jumped sharply, usually because of a one-off supply shock.","","A sudden spike in world oil prices fits this exactly: petrol and other fuel prices surge and pull headline CPI up. Because these are extreme movements, the trimmed mean leaves them out, so underlying inflation changes little.","","- A fall in oil prices would push headline inflation **below** underlying inflation, the opposite of the pattern.","- Steady wage growth in services raises costs across a broad range of items. It lifts underlying inflation as well as headline inflation, so it would not open up a gap.","- Gradual rent rises are a broad, persistent price change that the trimmed mean keeps, so they raise both measures."].join(`
`)})},{variants:he({question:["World prices rise sharply for components that Australian manufacturers import, such as microchips and machine parts. Manufacturers keep importing roughly the same quantity of these components.","","All else being equal, what is the most likely effect on the Australian dollar and on prices in Australia?"].join(`
`),choices:["The dollar appreciates and domestic prices rise","The dollar depreciates and domestic prices fall","The dollar depreciates and domestic prices rise","The dollar appreciates and domestic prices fall"],correctChoiceIndex:2,explanation:["**Exchange rate.** Importers pay for the components in foreign currency. If prices rise while quantities stay about the same, Australian firms spend more on imports, so they must sell more Australian dollars to buy foreign currency. The supply of Australian dollars in the FOREX market increases, and the dollar **depreciates**.","","**Domestic prices.** The components are intermediate goods, meaning inputs into production. Higher input costs raise production costs for Australian manufacturers, who pass some of the increase on as higher prices. This is cost-push (imported) inflation, so domestic prices **rise**. The depreciation adds to this, because it makes all imports dearer in Australian dollars.","","Any option with an appreciation gets the currency effect backwards, since more Australian dollars are being sold, not bought. Any option with falling prices ignores the higher cost of inputs."].join(`
`)})},{variants:he({question:["The table shows external data for an imaginary economy.","","| Item | Year 1 (AUD billion) | Year 2 (AUD billion) |","| --- | ---: | ---: |","| Gross domestic product (GDP) | 2,000 | 2,400 |","| Net foreign debt | 900 | 1,000 |","| Net foreign equity | 300 | 320 |","| Current account balance | −80 | −72 |","","Which statement correctly describes the change in the external stability of this economy between Year 1 and Year 2?"].join(`
`),choices:["Net foreign liabilities rose as a percentage of GDP, indicating weaker external stability.","The current account deficit rose as a percentage of GDP, indicating weaker external stability.","Both net foreign liabilities and the current account deficit fell as a percentage of GDP, indicating stronger external stability.","Net foreign liabilities fell as a percentage of GDP, indicating weaker external stability because less foreign capital is available."],correctChoiceIndex:2,explanation:["External stability is judged by the size of foreign liabilities and the current account deficit **relative to GDP**, because a larger economy can service larger debts.","","**Net foreign liabilities** are net foreign debt plus net foreign equity:","","$$\\text{Year 1: } \\frac{900 + 300}{2000} \\times 100 = 60\\% \\qquad \\text{Year 2: } \\frac{1000 + 320}{2400} \\times 100 = 55\\%$$","","**Current account deficit:**","","$$\\text{Year 1: } \\frac{80}{2000} \\times 100 = 4\\% \\qquad \\text{Year 2: } \\frac{72}{2400} \\times 100 = 3\\%$$","","Both ratios fell, so the economy relies less on foreign funding relative to its size, and external stability has **improved**.","","- The claim that net foreign liabilities rose as a percentage of GDP comes from comparing dollar amounts (1,200 rising to 1,320) without dividing by GDP, which grew faster.","- The current account deficit fell from 4% to 3% of GDP, so the claim that it rose is wrong.","- Lower net foreign liabilities relative to GDP mean a lighter burden of interest and dividend payments, so they signal stronger, not weaker, external stability."].join(`
`)})},{variants:he({question:["The table shows data for an imaginary economy whose exports are mainly minerals and farm products.","","| Year | Trade-weighted index | Terms of trade index |","| --- | ---: | ---: |","| 1 | 72 | 118 |","| 2 | 66 | 104 |","","Which event best explains the changes in both indexes between Year 1 and Year 2?"].join(`
`),choices:["Domestic interest rates rise relative to interest rates overseas","World prices for the economy’s main commodity exports rise sharply","Foreign firms greatly increase their direct investment in the economy","A slowdown in major trading partners reduces demand for the economy’s commodity exports"],correctChoiceIndex:3,explanation:["Both indexes fell. The trade-weighted index (TWI) dropped from 72 to 66, so the currency depreciated against a basket of trading partner currencies. The terms of trade dropped from 118 to 104, so export prices fell relative to import prices. The right event must explain both.","","A slowdown among major trading partners cuts demand for the economy’s commodity exports. Lower demand pushes down world commodity prices, so export prices fall and the terms of trade worsen. Foreign buyers also need less of the domestic currency to pay for exports, so demand for the currency falls and the TWI declines.","","- Higher domestic interest rates attract capital inflows, which raise demand for the currency and push the TWI **up**.","- Rising commodity export prices would **improve** the terms of trade and, by raising demand for the currency, lift the TWI.","- More foreign direct investment also increases demand for the currency, so the TWI would rise."].join(`
`)})},{variants:he({question:["The table shows nominal GDP and the consumer price index (CPI) for an imaginary economy.","","| Year | Nominal GDP (AUD billion) | CPI |","| --- | ---: | ---: |","| 1 | 1,500 | 150.0 |","| 2 | 1,650 | 159.0 |","","Which statement about Year 2 is correct?"].join(`
`),choices:["Real GDP grew by 10.0% and the inflation rate was 9.0%","Real GDP grew by 3.8% and the inflation rate was 6.0%","Real GDP grew by 3.8% and the inflation rate was 9.0%","Real GDP grew by 10.0% and the inflation rate was 6.0%"],correctChoiceIndex:1,explanation:["**Inflation** is the percentage change in the CPI, not the change in index points:","","$$\\frac{159 - 150}{150} \\times 100 = 6.0\\%$$","","**Real GDP** removes the effect of price changes. Divide nominal GDP by the CPI expressed as a proportion of 100:","","$$\\text{Year 1: } \\frac{1500}{1.50} = 1000 \\qquad \\text{Year 2: } \\frac{1650}{1.59} \\approx 1037.7$$","","$$\\text{Real growth} = \\frac{1037.7 - 1000}{1000} \\times 100 \\approx 3.8\\%$$","","Quick check: nominal GDP grew by 10% and prices by 6%, and $1.10 \\div 1.06 \\approx 1.038$, which confirms real growth of about 3.8%.","","- 10.0% is the growth in **nominal** GDP, which includes the rise in prices as well as the rise in output.","- 9.0% is the rise in the CPI measured in index points (159 minus 150). Index points equal a percentage only when the starting value is 100."].join(`
