`)],sampleAnswer:["Look at the short-wavelength end of the graph, where the two curves disagree.","","- **Model P** is the classical wave model. It assumes that the energy of the","  radiation is continuous, so any amount of energy can be emitted at any","  frequency. The predicted intensity then keeps increasing as the wavelength","  gets shorter, so the curve heads off the top of the graph. This is the",'  "ultraviolet catastrophe".',"- **Model Q** is Planck's model. It assumes that energy is emitted in discrete","  quanta of size $E = hf$. Short wavelengths mean high frequencies, so each","  quantum needs a lot of energy and few are emitted. The intensity therefore","  peaks and then drops towards zero at short wavelengths.","","Model Q matches measured black-body spectra, which is evidence for the","quantisation of energy."].join(`
