`),sampleAnswer:["**Galvanometer $G_1$:**","","After the switch closes, a steady current flows through solenoid 1 from the battery.","Because this is a battery, the current is DC, so once the current has built up,","$G_1$ shows a **constant deflection** in one direction.","","**Galvanometer $G_2$:**","","As the switch closes, the current in solenoid 1 builds up from zero to its steady","value. This **changing** current produces a **changing magnetic flux** through","solenoid 2 (by Faraday's law), inducing an EMF in solenoid 2.","","$G_2$ shows a **brief deflection** (momentary kick) and then returns to zero","once the current in solenoid 1 has reached its steady value. At that stage the","magnetic field is no longer changing, so there is no induced EMF.","","**Direction of induced current (Lenz's law):**","","The induced current in solenoid 2 opposes the increasing flux from solenoid 1.","So the two facing ends become like poles and the solenoids **repel** each other.","","**Motion of the carts:**","","The repulsive magnetic force pushes the carts apart while the flux is changing.","By conservation of momentum (system initially at rest):","","$$p_1 + p_2 = 0 \\implies m_1 v_1 = m_2 v_2$$","","Since $m_1 = 2m_2$:","","$$v_1 = \\frac{v_2}{2}$$","","Cart 1 moves away in one direction **at half the speed** of cart 2, while cart 2","moves in the opposite direction faster because it has the smaller mass."].join(`
