`),marks:4}]}}const oai=1,sai={id:"physics/2022/q33",version:1,variants:oai,syllabusTopics:[...nai],learningTopics:iai,generate:aai},rai=["PH-M6-1"],lai=["magnetic force on moving charges","direction of deflection in a magnetic field","charge-to-mass ratio and radius of curvature"];function cai(t){return{question:["Three particles X, Y, and Z enter a uniform magnetic field directed into","the page. All three particles enter with the same speed.","","- Particles X and Y curve upward (toward the top of the page).","- Particle Z curves downward (toward the bottom of the page).","- Particle X has a smaller radius of curvature than particle Y.","","Explain the paths of all three particles, referring to the charge,","mass, and charge-to-mass ratio of each particle."].join(`
`),sampleAnswer:["**Particles X and Y — positive charges, curving upward:**","","The magnetic force on a moving charge is given by $\\mathbf{F} = q\\mathbf{v} \\times \\mathbf{B}$.","Since both X and Y curve in the same direction (upward), they must carry the","**same sign of charge**. Given the direction of curvature (upward, with $\\mathbf{B}$","into the page and velocity to the right), the right-hand rule gives an upward","force for **positive charges**. So X and Y are positively charged.","","**Particle Z — negative charge, curving downward:**","","Particle Z curves in the **opposite direction** to X and Y.","A negative charge experiences a force opposite to that for a positive charge","under the same conditions. Therefore, Z is **negatively charged**.","","**Radius of curvature:**","","For a charged particle moving in a magnetic field, the radius is:","$$r = \\frac{mv}{qB}$$","","Since all particles enter with the same speed $v$, and $B$ is constant:","","$$r \\propto \\frac{m}{q}$$","","Particle X has a **smaller radius** than particle Y, so X has a **smaller","charge-to-mass ratio $m/q$** (or larger $q/m$).","","This means either:","- X has a smaller mass than Y (same charge), or","- X has a larger charge than Y (same mass), or","- a combination of both.","","The tighter curvature of X compared with Y means that the magnetic force","provides a centripetal acceleration over a shorter radius, consistent with","a smaller $m/q$ ratio for X."].join(`
`),marks:7}}const hai=1,dai={id:"physics/2022/q34",version:1,variants:hai,syllabusTopics:[...rai],learningTopics:lai,generate:cai},uai=["PH-M5-3","PH-M5-2"],mai=["orbital mechanics","gravitational force","centripetal force"];function pai(t){return{question:["A capsule of mass $m = 1.2 \\times 10^4$ kg orbits the International Space","Station (ISS) in a circular orbit.","","The ISS has mass $M = 4.2 \\times 10^5$ kg.","The orbital radius is $r = 200\\ \\mathrm{m}$ and the orbital speed is $v = 0.233\\ \\mathrm{m\\ s^{-1}}$.","","Test the hypothesis that the gravitational attraction between the capsule","and the ISS provides the centripetal force for the capsule's orbit.","","$(G = 6.67 \\times 10^{-11}\\ \\mathrm{N\\ m^2\\ kg^{-2}})$"].join(`
`),sampleAnswer:["**Step 1: Calculate the centripetal force required for circular motion.**","","$$F_c = \\frac{mv^2}{r} = \\frac{1.2 \\times 10^4 \\times (0.233)^2}{200}$$","","$$= \\frac{1.2 \\times 10^4 \\times 0.0543}{200} = \\frac{651.6}{200} \\approx 3.3 \\ \\text{N}$$","","**Step 2: Calculate the gravitational force between the capsule and ISS.**","","$$F_G = \\frac{GMm}{r^2} = \\frac{6.67 \\times 10^{-11} \\times 4.2 \\times 10^5 \\times 1.2 \\times 10^4}{(200)^2}$$","","$$= \\frac{6.67 \\times 10^{-11} \\times 5.04 \\times 10^9}{4.0 \\times 10^4}$$","","$$= \\frac{3.36 \\times 10^{-1}}{4.0 \\times 10^4} \\approx 8.4 \\times 10^{-6} \\ \\text{N}$$","","**Step 3: Compare $F_c$ and $F_G$.**","","| Force | Value |","|-------|-------|","| Required centripetal force $F_c$ | 3.3 N |","| Gravitational force from ISS $F_G$ | $8.4 \\times 10^{-6}$ N |","","$F_c \\approx 3.3$ N is approximately **400 000 times greater** than $F_G \\approx 8.4 \\times 10^{-6}$ N.","","**Conclusion:**","","The gravitational attraction between the capsule and the ISS is far too small","to provide the centripetal force needed for this orbit.","The hypothesis is **rejected** — gravity from the ISS does not account for","the circular motion of the capsule.","","*(The capsule and ISS are actually in the same orbit around Earth, and it is","Earth's gravity that keeps them both in orbit — not their mutual attraction.)*"].join(`
