`),choices:i,sampleAnswer:{correctChoiceIndex:e,explanation:["Start from Hubble's law, $v = H_0 d$. For nearby galaxies this is a straight line","through the origin, and the gradient is the **present** expansion rate.","","The key idea is **look-back time**. Light from a very distant galaxy set out billions","of years ago, so we measure that galaxy as it was when the universe was younger. If","the expansion is speeding up, it was **slower** in the past. Distant galaxies","therefore show smaller recession velocities than the straight line from nearby","galaxies predicts. Put another way, the supernovae at a given velocity were fainter,","and so further away, than expected.","","On the graph, the data follow a straight line at small distances and then bend","**below** it at large distances. The gradient gets smaller, so the curve flattens out.","","Why the other graphs fail:","- A straight line through the origin is the constant-rate expansion that the","  supernova results ruled out.","- A curve that gets steeper would mean distant galaxies (seen in the past) moved","  faster than the present rate predicts. That describes an expansion that has","  been **slowing down**.","- A falling line would mean more distant galaxies recede more slowly. That","  contradicts Hubble's observations even for nearby galaxies."].join(`
