`),parts:[{question:"Explain why a hydrogen-rich material such as paraffin wax was placed in the path of the unknown radiation.",sampleAnswer:["The unknown radiation was neutral, so it did not ionise the gas in the","detectors of the time and could not be detected directly. Paraffin wax is","full of hydrogen nuclei, which are single protons. When the radiation struck","the wax it knocked some of these protons out.","","Protons are charged, so they **can** be detected, and their energies and","ranges can be measured. The wax therefore turned an invisible radiation into","a measurable one. The proton energies gave the information needed to work","out the mass of whatever had struck them."].join(`
`),marks:2},{question:"Outline what Chadwick concluded about the radiation, and how this changed the accepted model of the atom.",sampleAnswer:["**Conclusion about the radiation.** Others had suggested it was high-energy","gamma radiation. However, gamma photons could not give the protons as much","energy as was measured without breaking conservation of energy and momentum.","Chadwick treated the event as a collision between particles. He showed that","the results fit if the radiation is a stream of **neutral particles with","about the same mass as a proton**. He called these particles **neutrons**.","","**Change to the model.** Before this, the nucleus was thought to contain only","protons (with some electrons to cancel charge), which could not account for","atomic masses. After Chadwick, the nucleus was understood to be made of","**protons and neutrons**. The atomic number counts the protons, and the mass","number counts protons plus neutrons. This explained why most atoms have","about twice as much mass as their charge alone would suggest, and it","explained isotopes as atoms with different numbers of neutrons."].join(`
`),marks:3},{question:["Bohr's model of the atom assumed that electrons can occupy only certain","orbits, but it gave no physical reason for this. Explain how de Broglie's","proposal about the nature of the electron provided one."].join(`
`),sampleAnswer:["**The problem.** Bohr simply *postulated* that an electron's angular momentum","is quantised ($mvr = nh/2\\pi$), so that only certain orbit radii and energy","levels are allowed. He also assumed that electrons in these orbits do not","radiate, although classical physics says an accelerating charge should lose","energy and spiral into the nucleus. Neither assumption was explained.","","**De Broglie's idea.** He proposed that every moving particle has a","wavelength $\\lambda = h/mv$, so an electron has wave properties.","","**How this explains the allowed orbits.** An electron wave going round the","nucleus must join up smoothly with itself. It must form a **standing wave**,","so a whole number of wavelengths fits around the orbit:","$$2\\pi r = n\\lambda = \\frac{nh}{mv} \\implies mvr = \\frac{nh}{2\\pi}$$","This is exactly Bohr's quantisation condition, now derived instead of assumed.","For other radii the wave would interfere destructively with itself, so those","orbits cannot exist.","","**Why no radiation.** A standing wave does not change with time. The electron","in an allowed orbit is in a stable state and does not continuously lose energy.","It only emits or absorbs a photon when it moves between two allowed states.","","(Electron diffraction experiments later confirmed de Broglie's wavelength.)"].join(`
