`),parts:[{question:["Describe how the leads must connect the two conductors to the supply","so that the currents in P and Q are different in size and the","conductors push each other apart. You may include a labelled sketch."].join(`
`),sampleAnswer:["**Parallel connection.** Each conductor is connected directly across the","supply terminals, one end of each to the positive terminal and the other","end to the negative terminal. Both conductors then have the same voltage","across them, but because they are different metals they have different","resistances, so $I = V/R$ gives each a different current. (In series","they would be forced to carry the same current.)","","**Opposite current directions.** Parallel conductors with currents in the","same direction attract; with currents in opposite directions they repel.","So the positive terminal must go to the left-hand end of P but to the","right-hand end of Q (or the reverse), making the currents antiparallel."].join(`
`),marks:2},{question:["The current in P is larger than the current in Q.","","A student claims:","",'*"Q moved further because P carries the bigger current, so P pushes on Q','harder than Q pushes on P."*',"","Evaluate the student's claim, using relevant physics principles."].join(`
`),sampleAnswer:["The claim is **not valid**: its conclusion about the forces is wrong,","even though its observation (Q moved further) is correct.","","**Size of the force.** The force per unit length between two parallel","conductors is","$$\\frac{F}{l} = \\frac{\\mu_0 I_P I_Q}{2\\pi r}$$","The force depends on the **product** of the two currents. Swapping which","conductor carries the larger current does not change it, so a larger","current in P does not give a larger force on Q.","","**Newton's third law.** The force P exerts on Q and the force Q exerts on","P are an action–reaction pair: equal in size and opposite in direction,","at every instant.","","**What does explain the result.** Both conductors feel the same size","force for the same time. By Newton's second law $a = F/m$, the conductor","with the **smaller mass** has the larger acceleration and so moves","further in the same time. Q must therefore be lighter than P (for","example, a less dense metal or a thinner rod).","","**Judgement.** The student correctly links the motion to the magnetic","force between the currents, but wrongly assumes the forces are unequal.","The difference in displacement comes from a difference in mass, not a","difference in force."].join(`
