`),wrongExplanations:{"Experimental production and detection of electromagnetic waves":"Hertz first produced and detected EM waves experimentally (1886–88), not Maxwell.","Double-slit experiment demonstrating interference of light":"Young performed the double-slit experiment (1801), demonstrating wave interference of light.","Measurement of the speed of light in water":"Foucault measured the speed of light in water (1850), confirming the wave model."}},{scientist:"Hertz",correctContribution:"Experimental production and detection of electromagnetic waves",correctPos:2,correctExplanation:["Hertz (1886–1888) was the first to **experimentally** produce and detect","electromagnetic waves, using a spark-gap transmitter and a loop-wire detector.","","He demonstrated that the waves reflected, refracted, and interfered just","as Maxwell had predicted, confirming the electromagnetic theory of light.","His contribution was **experimental**, not theoretical."].join(`
`),wrongExplanations:{"Theoretical prediction of the speed of electromagnetic waves":"Maxwell predicted the speed of EM waves theoretically (c = 1/√(μ₀ε₀)), not Hertz.","Double-slit experiment demonstrating interference of light":"Young performed the double-slit experiment (1801), which predates Hertz by nearly a century.","Measurement of the speed of light in water":"Foucault measured the speed of light in water (1850), confirming the wave model."}},{scientist:"Young",correctContribution:"Double-slit experiment demonstrating interference of light",correctPos:3,correctExplanation:["Young's double-slit experiment (1801) showed that light passing through","two narrow slits produces an **interference pattern** of alternating bright","and dark fringes on a screen.","","Interference is a wave phenomenon — particles cannot produce such a pattern.","Young's result was strong evidence that **light behaves as a wave**,","contradicting Newton's corpuscular (particle) model."].join(`
